WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.536 --> 00:02.986 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference July 28,

00:02.986 --> 00:04.316 align:middle
2021 CHAIR POWELL.

00:04.316 --> 00:04.676 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:05.286 --> 00:08.066 align:middle
At the Federal Reserve,
we are strongly committed

00:08.066 --> 00:09.986 align:middle
to achieving the
monetary policy goals

00:09.986 --> 00:11.296 align:middle
that Congress has given us:

00:11.476 --> 00:13.486 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability.

00:14.766 --> 00:17.506 align:middle
Today the Federal Open Market
Committee kept interest rates

00:17.606 --> 00:20.346 align:middle
near zero and maintained
our asset purchases.

00:21.436 --> 00:23.626 align:middle
These measures, along
with our strong guidance

00:23.626 --> 00:25.436 align:middle
on interest rates and
on our balance sheet,

00:25.986 --> 00:28.236 align:middle
will ensure that monetary
policy will continue

00:28.236 --> 00:30.856 align:middle
to support the economy until
the recovery is complete.

00:32.646 --> 00:34.046 align:middle
Progress in vaccinations

00:34.046 --> 00:37.576 align:middle
and unprecedented fiscal policy
actions are also providing

00:37.576 --> 00:39.216 align:middle
strong support to the recovery.

00:40.536 --> 00:43.406 align:middle
Indicators of economic activity
and employment have continued

00:43.406 --> 00:47.166 align:middle
to strengthen, and real GDP
this year appears to be on track

00:47.166 --> 00:50.016 align:middle
to post its fastest rate
of increase in decades.

00:51.076 --> 00:53.296 align:middle
Much of this rapid growth
reflects the continued

00:53.296 --> 00:55.806 align:middle
bounceback in activity
from depressed levels.

00:56.216 --> 00:59.556 align:middle
The sectors most
adversely affected

00:59.556 --> 01:01.326 align:middle
by the pandemic have
shown improvement

01:01.786 --> 01:03.316 align:middle
but have not fully recovered.

01:04.346 --> 01:07.526 align:middle
Household spending is rising
at an especially rapid pace,

01:07.726 --> 01:10.266 align:middle
boosted by the ongoing
reopening of the economy

01:10.436 --> 01:11.976 align:middle
and ongoing policy support.

01:13.346 --> 01:15.486 align:middle
The housing sector
remains very strong,

01:15.486 --> 01:18.226 align:middle
and business investment is
increasing at a solid pace.

01:19.286 --> 01:22.306 align:middle
In some industries, near-term
supply constraints are

01:22.306 --> 01:23.416 align:middle
restraining activity.

01:24.276 --> 01:26.076 align:middle
These constraints are
particularly acute

01:26.156 --> 01:29.796 align:middle
in the motor vehicle industry,
where worldwide shortages

01:29.846 --> 01:33.236 align:middle
of semiconductors have
sharply curtailed production

01:33.236 --> 01:34.226 align:middle
so far this year.

01:35.616 --> 01:37.436 align:middle
As with overall economic
activity,

01:37.616 --> 01:40.116 align:middle
conditions in the labor market
have continued to improve.

01:41.216 --> 01:45.496 align:middle
Demand for labor is very strong,
and employment rose 850,000

01:45.496 --> 01:49.166 align:middle
in June, with the leisure and
hospitality sector continuing

01:49.166 --> 01:50.696 align:middle
to post notable gains.

01:51.716 --> 01:54.386 align:middle
Nonetheless, the labor
market has a ways to go.

01:55.626 --> 02:01.286 align:middle
The unemployment rate in
June-in June was 5.9 percent,

02:01.386 --> 02:03.786 align:middle
and this figure understates
the shortfall in employment,

02:03.786 --> 02:07.146 align:middle
particularly as participation in
the labor market has not moved

02:07.146 --> 02:08.866 align:middle
up from the low rates
that have prevailed

02:08.866 --> 02:10.146 align:middle
for most of the past year.

02:11.446 --> 02:14.486 align:middle
Factors related to the pandemic,
such as caregiving needs,

02:14.596 --> 02:18.196 align:middle
ongoing fears of the virus, and
unemployment insurance payments,

02:18.676 --> 02:20.466 align:middle
appear to be weighing
on employment growth.

02:21.566 --> 02:23.676 align:middle
These factors should
wane in coming months,

02:23.856 --> 02:25.686 align:middle
leading to strong
gains in employment.

02:26.176 --> 02:30.676 align:middle
The economic downturn has not
fallen equally on all Americans,

02:30.976 --> 02:33.246 align:middle
and those least able to
shoulder the burden have been

02:33.246 --> 02:33.906 align:middle
hardest hit.

02:34.606 --> 02:37.796 align:middle
In particular, despite
progress, joblessness continues

02:37.796 --> 02:40.336 align:middle
to fall disproportionately
on lower-wage workers

02:40.336 --> 02:43.956 align:middle
in the service sector and on
African Americans and Hispanics.

02:45.106 --> 02:48.166 align:middle
Inflation has increased notably
and will likely remain elevated

02:48.166 --> 02:49.946 align:middle
in coming months
before moderating.

02:50.196 --> 02:53.996 align:middle
As the economy continues to
reopen and spending rebounds,

02:54.176 --> 02:57.476 align:middle
we are seeing upward pressure
on prices, particularly

02:57.476 --> 02:58.846 align:middle
because supply bottlenecks

02:58.846 --> 03:02.016 align:middle
in some sectors have limited how
quickly production can respond

03:02.016 --> 03:02.826 align:middle
in the near term.

03:03.986 --> 03:06.696 align:middle
These bottleneck effects have
been larger than anticipated,

03:07.106 --> 03:09.826 align:middle
but as these transitory
supply effects abate,

03:10.326 --> 03:13.546 align:middle
inflation is expected to drop
back toward our longer-run goal.

03:14.516 --> 03:17.266 align:middle
Very low readings from early
in the pandemic as well

03:17.266 --> 03:19.586 align:middle
as the pass-through of past
increases in oil prices

03:19.586 --> 03:22.766 align:middle
to consumer energy prices also
contribute to the increase,

03:23.236 --> 03:26.636 align:middle
although these base effects and
energy effects are receding.

03:27.216 --> 03:31.276 align:middle
The process of reopening the
economy is unprecedented,

03:31.796 --> 03:34.056 align:middle
as was the shutdown at
the onset of the pandemic.

03:34.946 --> 03:37.196 align:middle
As the reopening
continues, bottlenecks,

03:37.196 --> 03:40.196 align:middle
hiring difficulties, and other
constraints could continue

03:40.196 --> 03:43.916 align:middle
to limit how quickly supply can
adjust, raising the possibility

03:43.916 --> 03:45.676 align:middle
that inflation could
turn out to be higher

03:45.986 --> 03:49.146 align:middle
and more persistent
than we expect.

03:49.146 --> 03:52.786 align:middle
Our new framework for monetary
policy emphasizes the importance

03:52.786 --> 03:56.346 align:middle
of having well-anchored
inflation expectations, both to,

03:56.346 --> 03:59.486 align:middle
to foster price stability
and to enhance our ability

03:59.486 --> 04:01.016 align:middle
to promote our broad-based

04:01.016 --> 04:03.356 align:middle
and inclusive maximum-employment
goal.

04:04.326 --> 04:06.936 align:middle
Indicators of long-term
inflation expectations appear

04:06.936 --> 04:09.586 align:middle
broadly consistent with our
longer-run inflation goal

04:09.586 --> 04:10.256 align:middle
of 2 percent.

04:11.306 --> 04:13.466 align:middle
If we saw signs that
the path of inflation

04:13.466 --> 04:16.576 align:middle
or longer-term inflation
expectations were moving

04:16.576 --> 04:19.166 align:middle
materially and persistently
beyond levels consistent

04:19.166 --> 04:22.276 align:middle
with our goal, we'd be prepared
to adjust the stance of policy.

04:22.816 --> 04:26.306 align:middle
The effects of the pandemic
on the economy have continued

04:26.306 --> 04:29.796 align:middle
to diminish, but risks to
the economic outlook remain.

04:31.156 --> 04:34.636 align:middle
Progress on vaccinations has
limited the spread of COVID-19.

04:35.056 --> 04:37.276 align:middle
However, the pace of
vaccinations has slowed,

04:37.656 --> 04:38.666 align:middle
and the Delta strain

04:38.666 --> 04:41.196 align:middle
of the virus is spreading
quickly in some areas.

04:42.056 --> 04:42.976 align:middle
Continued progress

04:42.976 --> 04:45.286 align:middle
on vaccinations would
support a return

04:45.286 --> 04:47.186 align:middle
to more normal economic
conditions.

04:47.736 --> 04:51.966 align:middle
The Fed's policy actions have
been guided by our mandate

04:51.966 --> 04:54.326 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

04:54.326 --> 04:57.266 align:middle
for the American people, along
with our responsibilities

04:57.266 --> 04:59.256 align:middle
to promote the stability
of the financial system.

05:00.576 --> 05:03.206 align:middle
As the Committee reiterated
in today's policy statement,

05:03.746 --> 05:06.386 align:middle
with inflation having run
persistently below 2 percent,

05:06.756 --> 05:09.626 align:middle
we will aim to achieve inflation
moderately above 2 percent

05:09.626 --> 05:13.166 align:middle
for some time so that inflation
averages 2 percent over time

05:13.536 --> 05:16.076 align:middle
and longer-term inflation
expectations remain well

05:16.076 --> 05:17.206 align:middle
anchored at 2 percent.

05:18.506 --> 05:20.506 align:middle
We expect to maintain
an accommodative stance

05:20.506 --> 05:22.516 align:middle
of monetary policy
until these employment

05:22.516 --> 05:24.306 align:middle
and inflation outcomes
are achieved.

05:24.816 --> 05:28.056 align:middle
With regard to interest
rates, we continue to expect

05:28.056 --> 05:30.476 align:middle
that it will be appropriate
to maintain the current 0

05:30.476 --> 05:33.476 align:middle
to ¼ percent target range
for the federal funds rate

05:33.826 --> 05:36.266 align:middle
until labor market conditions
have reached level-levels

05:36.266 --> 05:39.256 align:middle
consistent with the Committee's
assessment of maximum employment

05:39.746 --> 05:42.106 align:middle
and inflation has risen to
2 percent and is on track

05:42.106 --> 05:45.786 align:middle
to moderately exceed 2
percent for some time.

05:45.786 --> 05:48.596 align:middle
In addition, we are
continuing to-continuing

05:48.596 --> 05:50.706 align:middle
to increase our holdings
of Treasury securities

05:51.136 --> 05:55.136 align:middle
by at least $80 billion
per month and of agency MBS

05:55.186 --> 05:57.376 align:middle
by at least $40 billion
per month

05:57.826 --> 06:00.746 align:middle
until substantial further
progress has been made toward

06:00.746 --> 06:03.276 align:middle
our maximum-employment
and price-stability goals.

06:04.956 --> 06:07.036 align:middle
Our asset purchases have
been a critical tool.

06:07.586 --> 06:09.376 align:middle
They helped preserve
financial stability

06:09.376 --> 06:11.296 align:middle
and market functioning
early in the pandemic

06:11.976 --> 06:14.526 align:middle
and since then have helped
foster accommodative financial

06:14.526 --> 06:16.106 align:middle
conditions to support
the economy.

06:17.376 --> 06:19.476 align:middle
At our meeting that
concluded earlier today,

06:19.476 --> 06:20.396 align:middle
the Committee continued

06:20.396 --> 06:23.146 align:middle
to discuss the progress made
toward our-toward our goals

06:23.386 --> 06:26.116 align:middle
since the Committee adopted
its asset purchase guidance

06:26.116 --> 06:26.866 align:middle
last December.

06:27.806 --> 06:29.716 align:middle
We also reviewed
some considerations

06:29.716 --> 06:32.106 align:middle
around how our asset
purchases might be adjusted,

06:32.596 --> 06:34.526 align:middle
including their pace
and composition,

06:34.836 --> 06:37.426 align:middle
once economic conditions
warrant a change.

06:38.436 --> 06:40.716 align:middle
Participants expect that
the economy will continue

06:40.716 --> 06:43.726 align:middle
to move toward our standard of
"substantial further progress."

06:44.526 --> 06:45.376 align:middle
In coming meetings,

06:45.376 --> 06:48.156 align:middle
the Committee will again assess
the economy's progress toward

06:48.156 --> 06:51.026 align:middle
our goals, and the timing
of any change in the pace

06:51.026 --> 06:54.266 align:middle
of our asset purchases will
depend on the incoming data.

06:55.136 --> 06:58.776 align:middle
As we've said, we will provide
advance notice before making any

06:58.776 --> 07:00.006 align:middle
changes to our purchases.

07:00.536 --> 07:03.526 align:middle
On a final note, we
announced the establishment

07:03.526 --> 07:06.166 align:middle
of two standing repo
facilities, a domestic one

07:06.226 --> 07:09.476 align:middle
for primary dealers and
additional banks and another

07:09.476 --> 07:12.376 align:middle
for foreign and international
monetary authorities.

07:13.296 --> 07:16.286 align:middle
These facilities will serve
as backstops in money markets

07:16.286 --> 07:18.066 align:middle
to support the effective
implementation

07:18.066 --> 07:20.676 align:middle
of monetary policy and
smooth market functioning.

07:21.296 --> 07:25.416 align:middle
To conclude, we understand that
our actions affect communities,

07:25.416 --> 07:27.716 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

07:28.546 --> 07:30.966 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

07:31.986 --> 07:34.576 align:middle
We at the Fed will do everything
we can to support the economy

07:34.576 --> 07:36.566 align:middle
for as long as it takes
to complete the recovery.

07:37.636 --> 07:38.086 align:middle
Thank you.

07:38.256 --> 07:39.556 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

07:40.416 --> 07:41.466 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:44.356 --> 07:44.916 align:middle
Thank you.

07:44.916 --> 07:47.126 align:middle
Let's go to Steve
Liesman with CNBC.

07:47.126 --> 07:48.016 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

07:48.816 --> 07:50.596 align:middle
Thank you very much.

07:50.886 --> 07:53.836 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, I wonder if you
might be able to put some,

07:54.466 --> 07:58.056 align:middle
I don't know, numbers or maybe
more detail around this concept

07:58.056 --> 08:00.146 align:middle
of "substantial further
progress."

08:00.516 --> 08:04.196 align:middle
What counts as the progress
numerically, if you will, or,

08:04.196 --> 08:07.616 align:middle
or, or citing data, if
you could, that you cited

08:07.616 --> 08:08.966 align:middle
in the statement today?

08:09.226 --> 08:10.756 align:middle
And if you could
be more specific

08:10.756 --> 08:14.746 align:middle
about what substantial further
progress would look like and if

08:14.746 --> 08:17.126 align:middle
that would then lead
you to an announcement

08:17.126 --> 08:21.326 align:middle
of an actual reduction in
the purchases of your assets.

08:21.996 --> 08:22.996 align:middle
Thank you.

08:23.056 --> 08:24.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:24.526 --> 08:25.606 align:middle
Great. Thank you.

08:26.896 --> 08:29.216 align:middle
So more detail on
substantial further progress.

08:29.766 --> 08:33.296 align:middle
So let's talk about the
maximum-employment part of that.

08:33.826 --> 08:38.196 align:middle
As you know, with maximum
employment-unlike with,

08:38.296 --> 08:41.136 align:middle
with price stability, where we
can target a number of 2 percent

08:41.526 --> 08:43.546 align:middle
on average-with maximum
employment,

08:43.616 --> 08:45.616 align:middle
there isn't a single
number that we can target.

08:45.616 --> 08:48.646 align:middle
That the-we monitor
a broad range of, of,

08:48.646 --> 08:52.386 align:middle
of data about different
aspects of the labor market.

08:52.386 --> 08:54.536 align:middle
There's unemployment,
unemployment among different,

08:55.016 --> 08:56.836 align:middle
different age groups, and such.

08:56.836 --> 08:58.886 align:middle
There's participation.

08:58.886 --> 08:59.766 align:middle
There's wages.

08:59.766 --> 09:01.356 align:middle
There's all kinds of flow data.

09:01.356 --> 09:04.416 align:middle
And we look at all of it to
try to arrive at a picture

09:04.416 --> 09:06.746 align:middle
of what-of what is
maximum employment.

09:06.746 --> 09:10.336 align:middle
So there isn't-I
can't give you a, a,

09:10.336 --> 09:14.186 align:middle
a set of numbers-for example, a
numerical threshold like we used

09:14.186 --> 09:16.876 align:middle
for a time back in
2012, I guess it was.

09:17.496 --> 09:18.596 align:middle
We didn't do that here.

09:18.596 --> 09:21.506 align:middle
What we said was substantial
further progress toward

09:21.506 --> 09:22.056 align:middle
our goals.

09:22.056 --> 09:25.046 align:middle
And what we said was, we
would keep, effectively,

09:25.046 --> 09:29.026 align:middle
we'd-we would give advance
warning as we-and, and,

09:29.386 --> 09:32.006 align:middle
you know, more and more
clarity-as, as we move forward.

09:32.006 --> 09:33.696 align:middle
And that's what we're
going to try to do.

09:33.696 --> 09:36.206 align:middle
So what would substantial
further progress be?

09:36.206 --> 09:39.096 align:middle
I'd say we have some
ground to cover on,

09:39.096 --> 09:40.946 align:middle
on the labor market side.

09:41.136 --> 09:44.046 align:middle
I think we're, we're
some way away from,

09:44.046 --> 09:48.156 align:middle
from having had substantial
further progress with,

09:49.556 --> 09:52.706 align:middle
with max-toward the
maximum-employment goal.

09:53.166 --> 09:55.026 align:middle
I would want to see
some strong job numbers.

09:55.286 --> 09:56.906 align:middle
And, and that's,
that's kind of the idea.

09:56.906 --> 09:58.316 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

09:58.816 --> 10:02.066 align:middle
If, if I could follow up,

10:02.066 --> 10:04.736 align:middle
you talked about one
side of the equation.

10:04.736 --> 10:07.246 align:middle
You didn't-does it mean you feel
like you've reached your goal

10:07.246 --> 10:08.596 align:middle
when it comes to
the inflation side?

10:08.596 --> 10:08.986 align:middle
Thank you.

10:08.986 --> 10:09.496 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:09.496 --> 10:13.696 align:middle
So inflation is running well
above our 2 percent objective

10:13.696 --> 10:16.446 align:middle
and has been for a few
months and is expected to run

10:17.136 --> 10:18.826 align:middle
up certainly above our objective

10:18.826 --> 10:21.236 align:middle
for a few months before we
believe it'll, it'll move back

10:21.236 --> 10:22.166 align:middle
down toward our objective.

10:22.576 --> 10:23.776 align:middle
The question whether we've met

10:23.776 --> 10:25.586 align:middle
that objective formally
is really one

10:25.586 --> 10:26.726 align:middle
for the Committee to make.

10:26.726 --> 10:28.846 align:middle
I can't-I can't do
that by myself.

10:29.476 --> 10:30.966 align:middle
But it's clear that,
at this time,

10:31.086 --> 10:33.356 align:middle
inflation is actually
running above 2 percent.

10:33.356 --> 10:35.976 align:middle
And, and, again, has
been and will be,

10:35.976 --> 10:38.926 align:middle
at least we expect it will, in
coming months before returning

10:39.686 --> 10:40.786 align:middle
down toward our target.

10:40.786 --> 10:41.386 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

10:43.486 --> 10:44.236 align:middle
Thank you.

10:44.236 --> 10:45.496 align:middle
Ann Saphir with Reuters.

10:45.496 --> 10:46.176 align:middle
ANN SAPHIR.

10:51.026 --> 10:52.676 align:middle
Hello. Can you hear me?

10:52.776 --> 10:53.226 align:middle
[No response] Hello.

10:53.226 --> 10:55.526 align:middle
Can you hear me?

10:55.706 --> 10:57.406 align:middle
I'm sorry.

10:57.406 --> 10:57.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:58.266 --> 11:00.226 align:middle
Yes, yes. We can hear you.

11:00.226 --> 11:00.956 align:middle
ANN SAPHIR.

11:00.956 --> 11:01.826 align:middle
I apologize.

11:01.826 --> 11:06.396 align:middle
I-just to follow up on the
question, I want to ask,

11:06.396 --> 11:09.906 align:middle
you know, is it-is it
correct to see this as the,

11:09.906 --> 11:13.206 align:middle
the start of the
advance notice process,

11:13.206 --> 11:17.486 align:middle
you know, before a taper?

11:17.486 --> 11:21.356 align:middle
And, and, and also, can you
speak to how the recent surge

11:21.356 --> 11:26.096 align:middle
in COVID factors into your
thinking on, on the taper?

11:26.396 --> 11:30.086 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

11:30.086 --> 11:32.276 align:middle
So, as you know, we're,
we're in a process now

11:32.276 --> 11:38.566 align:middle
where what we said is
that as-at this meeting

11:38.566 --> 11:41.506 align:middle
and in coming meetings,
we're going to be continuing

11:41.506 --> 11:45.626 align:middle
to assess the economy's
progress toward our goals

11:46.206 --> 11:47.376 align:middle
and give advance notice.

11:47.376 --> 11:49.526 align:middle
We'll be-try to provide
additional clarity

11:49.526 --> 11:53.136 align:middle
about our thinking, both in
the-in the postmeeting statement

11:53.136 --> 11:54.816 align:middle
and in the minutes and
in the public comments

11:54.816 --> 11:55.526 align:middle
that people make.

11:55.526 --> 11:58.736 align:middle
You know, our, our
approach here has been to be

11:58.736 --> 11:59.976 align:middle
as transparent as we can.

12:00.766 --> 12:03.356 align:middle
We have not reached substantial
further progress yet.

12:03.356 --> 12:05.546 align:middle
So we're, we're not
there, and we see our ways

12:05.546 --> 12:07.616 align:middle
as having some-we see
ourselves as having some ground

12:07.616 --> 12:09.396 align:middle
to cover to get there.

12:09.466 --> 12:12.636 align:middle
So that's what I would say.

12:12.756 --> 12:18.686 align:middle
In terms of-in terms of, of
COVID and the Delta strain,

12:18.686 --> 12:19.846 align:middle
I'll say a couple things.

12:20.386 --> 12:23.986 align:middle
Of course, it will have
significant health consequences

12:23.986 --> 12:24.486 align:middle
for many.

12:24.486 --> 12:26.696 align:middle
And we need to keep that in
mind before we start-before we

12:26.696 --> 12:28.866 align:middle
mention and move to
the economic questions.

12:29.576 --> 12:33.346 align:middle
This is a-rising cases in, in a
number of parts of the country,

12:33.346 --> 12:36.166 align:middle
and some forecasts are for them
to rise quite significantly.

12:36.166 --> 12:36.696 align:middle
We'll see.

12:37.296 --> 12:40.956 align:middle
What we've seen, though, is
with successive waves of,

12:41.076 --> 12:44.786 align:middle
of COVID over the past
year and some months now,

12:45.316 --> 12:48.056 align:middle
there has tended to be less
economic-less in the way

12:48.056 --> 12:50.176 align:middle
of economic implications
from each wave.

12:50.296 --> 12:53.726 align:middle
And we will see whether that is
the case with the Delta variety.

12:53.726 --> 12:57.236 align:middle
But it's, you know, it's
a-it's certainly a-not an

12:57.236 --> 12:59.006 align:middle
unreasonable expectation.

13:01.076 --> 13:04.236 align:middle
So it, it certainly is plausible
if people would pull back

13:04.236 --> 13:06.606 align:middle
from some activities because
of the risk of infection.

13:07.136 --> 13:09.946 align:middle
Dining out, traveling, schools
might-some schools might

13:09.946 --> 13:10.986 align:middle
not reopen.

13:11.586 --> 13:14.106 align:middle
We may just-we may see economic
effects from some of that,

13:14.106 --> 13:16.826 align:middle
or it might weigh on, on the
return to the labor market.

13:16.826 --> 13:21.036 align:middle
Some people might choose-again,
we, we don't have a strong sense

13:21.036 --> 13:22.086 align:middle
of how that might work out,

13:22.086 --> 13:24.916 align:middle
so we'll just be monitoring
it-monitoring it carefully.

13:24.916 --> 13:25.526 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

13:27.226 --> 13:27.886 align:middle
Thank you.

13:27.886 --> 13:29.816 align:middle
We'll go to Nick Timiraos
at the Wall Street Journal.

13:29.816 --> 13:31.746 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

13:32.896 --> 13:33.926 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

13:33.926 --> 13:35.656 align:middle
Nick Timiraos at the
Wall Street Journal.

13:36.536 --> 13:39.586 align:middle
In your opening statements
in March and April,

13:40.086 --> 13:42.866 align:middle
you noted that a
transitory rise in inflation

13:42.866 --> 13:45.536 align:middle
above 2 percent this year
would not meet the threshold

13:45.536 --> 13:48.626 align:middle
of moderately exceeding
2 percent for some time,

13:48.626 --> 13:50.006 align:middle
and I noticed you didn't repeat

13:50.006 --> 13:53.096 align:middle
that qualification
last month or today.

13:53.646 --> 13:56.126 align:middle
And, and so, in your
view, has the rise

13:56.126 --> 13:58.346 align:middle
in inflation this
year met the threshold

13:58.746 --> 14:01.696 align:middle
of moderately exceeding
2 percent for some time?

14:02.136 --> 14:03.176 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:03.176 --> 14:05.866 align:middle
That, that would, again, be
a question for the Committee.

14:05.866 --> 14:09.386 align:middle
But I would really say, the
guidance that you're talking

14:09.386 --> 14:12.866 align:middle
about is really the guidance
to do with liftoff, right?

14:12.866 --> 14:14.406 align:middle
That's-what the guidance
is for liftoff,

14:14.406 --> 14:16.616 align:middle
we had to have labor
market conditions consistent

14:16.616 --> 14:20.466 align:middle
with full employment, inflation
at 2 percent and on track

14:20.466 --> 14:22.706 align:middle
to run-to run moderately
above 2 percent for some time.

14:23.156 --> 14:25.736 align:middle
It really isn't relevant now.

14:25.886 --> 14:27.596 align:middle
It-because we're
really-we're looking at,

14:27.596 --> 14:29.216 align:middle
at tapering asset purchases.

14:29.216 --> 14:31.056 align:middle
We're clearly a ways away

14:31.056 --> 14:33.476 align:middle
from considering
raising interest rates.

14:33.476 --> 14:36.056 align:middle
It's not something that, that is
on our radar screen right now.

14:36.776 --> 14:40.846 align:middle
You know, so when we get to that
question, when we start to get

14:40.846 --> 14:45.296 align:middle
to the question of, of liftoff,
which, which we are not at all

14:45.546 --> 14:48.826 align:middle
at now or near now, that's
when we'll ask that question.

14:49.086 --> 14:51.376 align:middle
That is when that, that will
become a real question for us.

14:51.486 --> 14:51.986 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:55.146 --> 14:55.686 align:middle
Thank you.

14:55.686 --> 14:56.896 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek at
the New York Times.

14:56.896 --> 14:57.586 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

14:57.586 --> 14:59.806 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

14:59.806 --> 15:01.956 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

15:01.956 --> 15:04.386 align:middle
I wonder if you could
talk a little bit

15:04.386 --> 15:07.706 align:middle
about [inaudible]
reacting [inaudible].

15:09.616 --> 15:11.266 align:middle
You know, does that affect,

15:14.166 --> 15:18.656 align:middle
affect how you're
thinking about tapering?

15:18.656 --> 15:20.406 align:middle
Does it affect how you're
thinking about, you know,

15:20.406 --> 15:21.566 align:middle
potential liftoff down the road?

15:21.566 --> 15:23.176 align:middle
And if you could, just talk
a little bit about that.

15:23.176 --> 15:23.406 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:23.866 --> 15:24.606 align:middle
Jeanna, I'm sorry.

15:24.606 --> 15:27.346 align:middle
For the first 15 seconds of
your question, you froze.

15:27.346 --> 15:28.536 align:middle
Could you-could you
say that again?

15:28.536 --> 15:29.246 align:middle
I apologize.

15:29.376 --> 15:29.766 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

15:29.766 --> 15:30.126 align:middle
Oh, yes. No.

15:30.126 --> 15:30.736 align:middle
Sorry. Yes.

15:30.736 --> 15:32.746 align:middle
So I was wondering if you
could talk a little bit

15:32.746 --> 15:37.456 align:middle
about how the divergence in
global growth and the sort

15:37.456 --> 15:38.916 align:middle
of multispeed recovery
we're seeing

15:38.916 --> 15:43.086 align:middle
around the world impacts how the
Fed thinks about its policies.

15:43.086 --> 15:45.496 align:middle
So do you take into account
that sort of multispeed recovery

15:45.496 --> 15:48.846 align:middle
when you're thinking about
tapering QE, and do you take,

15:48.846 --> 15:52.016 align:middle
take it into account when
you're thinking about liftoff?

15:52.016 --> 15:52.416 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:52.636 --> 15:55.876 align:middle
Thanks. So it's an-it's
an important feature

15:55.876 --> 15:57.936 align:middle
of this recovery is
how uneven it is.

15:57.936 --> 16:00.986 align:middle
And, in many cases,
that's related to the fact

16:00.986 --> 16:02.796 align:middle
that some countries have
had little in the way

16:02.796 --> 16:05.156 align:middle
of access to, to vaccines.

16:05.156 --> 16:07.466 align:middle
And so they're seeing
significant outbreaks,

16:07.466 --> 16:09.046 align:middle
and it's weighing on
economic activity,

16:09.046 --> 16:11.056 align:middle
whereas other countries-such
as the United States,

16:11.056 --> 16:13.306 align:middle
in particular-are having
a very strong rebound.

16:13.956 --> 16:17.936 align:middle
And, and now Europe is having
a stronger rebound as well.

16:17.936 --> 16:19.936 align:middle
So it's a feature
of our economy.

16:19.936 --> 16:21.676 align:middle
Now, how does it
affect our policy?

16:22.306 --> 16:24.976 align:middle
In, in a couple of
ways, potentially.

16:25.006 --> 16:29.486 align:middle
One just is that, in
general, economies-and

16:29.486 --> 16:31.746 align:middle
through financial markets
and through trade-are,

16:31.746 --> 16:33.336 align:middle
are deeply interconnected now.

16:33.766 --> 16:38.976 align:middle
And so a stronger global economy
will lead to more U.S. exports.

16:39.096 --> 16:41.846 align:middle
And, and, you know, that'll
help economic activity.

16:41.846 --> 16:44.446 align:middle
To the extent the
global economy is weak

16:44.916 --> 16:49.306 align:middle
and the United States is strong,
it'll-we'll wind up, you know,

16:49.306 --> 16:52.146 align:middle
we'll wind up exporting
some of our demand through,

16:52.146 --> 16:55.206 align:middle
through imports rather than
having, having a lot of exports.

16:55.206 --> 16:55.936 align:middle
That's one way.

16:56.246 --> 16:58.556 align:middle
Another way, though, is
more on the risk side.

16:58.556 --> 17:01.826 align:middle
And that is, as long as COVID
is running loose out there,

17:01.826 --> 17:05.316 align:middle
as long as there's time and
space for the development

17:05.316 --> 17:08.836 align:middle
of new strains, no one's
really finally safe.

17:08.836 --> 17:11.426 align:middle
These, these strains-there's
no reason they just can't keep

17:11.426 --> 17:14.746 align:middle
coming and one, you know, one
more powerful than the next.

17:14.746 --> 17:15.346 align:middle
We don't know that.

17:15.346 --> 17:18.506 align:middle
But that's, that's, that's
certainly a plausible outcome.

17:18.506 --> 17:22.786 align:middle
Now, as, as vaccinations rise,
we can nonetheless get back

17:22.786 --> 17:24.366 align:middle
to our economic activity.

17:24.366 --> 17:27.956 align:middle
But, you know, it, it is both
the right thing to do and,

17:27.956 --> 17:30.776 align:middle
and very much in our
interest to make sure

17:30.776 --> 17:33.106 align:middle
that vaccination happens broadly

17:33.106 --> 17:35.216 align:middle
around the world,
just for that reason.

17:35.216 --> 17:35.946 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:38.416 --> 17:39.166 align:middle
Thank you.

17:39.166 --> 17:40.816 align:middle
James at the FT.

17:41.656 --> 17:42.426 align:middle
JAMES POLITI.

17:43.076 --> 17:44.326 align:middle
Thank you very much.

17:45.476 --> 17:47.346 align:middle
Chair Powell, how would
you describe the risks

17:47.346 --> 17:49.546 align:middle
to the outlook on
inflation at the moment

17:49.676 --> 17:53.426 align:middle
after the last data came
out higher than expected?

17:53.426 --> 17:56.286 align:middle
Do you believe the risks are,
you know, tilted to the upside?

17:56.286 --> 17:59.036 align:middle
And does the Committee
share that view?

17:59.316 --> 18:00.566 align:middle
Or are they in balance?

18:00.566 --> 18:03.256 align:middle
Are you still worried that there
could be a hit to demand from,

18:03.656 --> 18:06.816 align:middle
from the Delta variant
that could tilt them sort

18:06.816 --> 18:08.106 align:middle
of to the downside again?

18:08.106 --> 18:10.136 align:middle
If you could give
us a sense of that,

18:10.136 --> 18:11.976 align:middle
that would be very helpful.

18:11.976 --> 18:12.486 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:12.486 --> 18:13.256 align:middle
Sure. I'd be glad to.

18:13.256 --> 18:15.706 align:middle
So if you look, again,
if you look at the,

18:15.706 --> 18:19.936 align:middle
the most recent inflation
report, what you see is

18:19.936 --> 18:22.126 align:middle
that it came in significantly
higher than expected.

18:22.126 --> 18:24.976 align:middle
But essentially all of
the overshoot can be tied

18:24.976 --> 18:26.316 align:middle
to a handful of categories.

18:26.316 --> 18:29.196 align:middle
It isn't the kind of
inflation that's spread broadly

18:29.196 --> 18:30.246 align:middle
across the economy.

18:30.666 --> 18:33.276 align:middle
It's new, used, and rental cars.

18:33.276 --> 18:34.206 align:middle
It's airplane tickets.

18:34.206 --> 18:34.846 align:middle
It's hotels.

18:34.846 --> 18:35.906 align:middle
And it's a couple
of other things.

18:36.236 --> 18:38.536 align:middle
And each of those has
a story attached to it

18:38.536 --> 18:40.796 align:middle
that is-that is really about
the reopening of the economy.

18:40.796 --> 18:45.396 align:middle
So we look at that, and we think
that those are temporary things

18:45.396 --> 18:47.806 align:middle
because the, the supply
side will respond.

18:47.806 --> 18:49.206 align:middle
The economy will adapt.

18:49.256 --> 18:51.556 align:middle
We have a very adaptive,
adaptable,

18:51.556 --> 18:54.006 align:middle
flexible economy
and labor market.

18:54.526 --> 18:56.846 align:middle
And it's a real-a real
asset that we have.

18:56.846 --> 18:59.286 align:middle
And so we think that inflation
should move down over time.

18:59.386 --> 19:02.866 align:middle
Now, we don't have any-much
confidence, let's say,

19:02.866 --> 19:04.536 align:middle
in the timing of
that or the size

19:04.536 --> 19:05.966 align:middle
of the effects in the near term.

19:05.966 --> 19:09.126 align:middle
I would say, in the near
term, that the, the,

19:09.126 --> 19:11.436 align:middle
the risks to inflation are
probably to the upside.

19:12.116 --> 19:16.696 align:middle
I, I, I have some confidence
in the-in the medium term,

19:16.696 --> 19:18.566 align:middle
that inflation will
move back down.

19:18.966 --> 19:21.566 align:middle
Again, it's hard to
say when that will be.

19:22.146 --> 19:24.686 align:middle
I, I, I will say,
though, that, you know,

19:24.686 --> 19:26.756 align:middle
we-inflation is half
of our mandate.

19:26.816 --> 19:28.436 align:middle
Price stability is
half of our mandate.

19:28.806 --> 19:32.986 align:middle
And if we were to see inflation
moving up to levels persistently

19:32.986 --> 19:37.976 align:middle
that were-that were
above, significantly,

19:37.976 --> 19:40.616 align:middle
materially above our
goal and particularly

19:40.616 --> 19:42.376 align:middle
if inflation expectations
were to move up,

19:42.376 --> 19:45.666 align:middle
we would use our tools
to guide inflation back

19:45.666 --> 19:46.506 align:middle
down to 2 percent.

19:46.506 --> 19:51.036 align:middle
So we won't have an extended
period of, of high inflation.

19:51.626 --> 19:55.376 align:middle
We think that, that some of it
will, will fall away naturally

19:55.376 --> 19:59.146 align:middle
as the process of reopening
the economy moves through.

19:59.146 --> 20:00.246 align:middle
And it could take some time.

20:00.766 --> 20:04.086 align:middle
In any case, we will use our
tools over time as appropriate

20:04.086 --> 20:06.676 align:middle
to make sure that
we do have inflation

20:06.676 --> 20:08.006 align:middle
that averages 2 percent
over time.

20:08.186 --> 20:08.646 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

20:11.036 --> 20:11.786 align:middle
Thank you.

20:11.786 --> 20:13.266 align:middle
Victoria at Politico.

20:14.956 --> 20:15.796 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

20:16.936 --> 20:17.746 align:middle
Hi, Mr. Chairman.

20:17.746 --> 20:21.236 align:middle
So bond market pricing
seems to suggest

20:21.236 --> 20:24.086 align:middle
that investors think the Fed
might eventually overtighten.

20:24.596 --> 20:26.956 align:middle
So my question is, do you
think that markets have bought

20:26.956 --> 20:28.546 align:middle
into the central
bank's new framework?

20:28.546 --> 20:31.806 align:middle
And how do you balance that with
managing inflation concerns?

20:31.936 --> 20:32.336 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:33.126 --> 20:36.416 align:middle
Well, so in terms of what's
been happening in bond markets,

20:36.416 --> 20:38.956 align:middle
I don't think there's
a real consensus on,

20:38.956 --> 20:40.816 align:middle
on what explains the moves

20:40.816 --> 20:44.566 align:middle
between the last
meeting and this meeting.

20:44.566 --> 20:46.786 align:middle
We've seen the long-term
yields go down significantly.

20:46.986 --> 20:49.146 align:middle
Some of it is a fall
in real yields,

20:49.146 --> 20:54.066 align:middle
which may have been connected
to-some speculate-connected

20:54.066 --> 20:58.326 align:middle
to sentiment around the, the
spread of the Delta variant

20:58.686 --> 21:00.986 align:middle
and concern about growth.

21:01.186 --> 21:03.956 align:middle
There was also some decline
in inflation compensation,

21:03.956 --> 21:05.976 align:middle
which has significantly
reversed.

21:06.646 --> 21:10.036 align:middle
And there, there are also
so-called technical factors,

21:10.036 --> 21:12.766 align:middle
which is where you put things
that you can't quite explain.

21:12.766 --> 21:16.746 align:middle
So I, I, I don't
see in any of that

21:16.956 --> 21:22.436 align:middle
that there is really anything
that challenges the credibility

21:22.436 --> 21:24.566 align:middle
of our framework, if, if
that's really your question.

21:24.996 --> 21:27.716 align:middle
We are committed to achieving
2 percent average inflation

21:27.716 --> 21:28.436 align:middle
over time.

21:29.086 --> 21:32.726 align:middle
What we said was
that, in particular,

21:32.806 --> 21:35.386 align:middle
when we see a very
strong labor market,

21:35.716 --> 21:38.346 align:middle
high levels of employment,
low levels of unemployment,

21:38.566 --> 21:40.636 align:middle
that won't be enough for
us to raise interest rates

21:40.636 --> 21:42.116 align:middle
until we see some inflation.

21:42.766 --> 21:45.176 align:middle
Of course, what we have today
is kind of the opposite.

21:45.176 --> 21:49.056 align:middle
We've got, you know, 7 or 8
million people, fewer people,

21:49.056 --> 21:51.826 align:middle
at work than were at
work before the pandemic.

21:52.316 --> 21:55.226 align:middle
So we're, you know,
we're, we're a ways away

21:55.226 --> 21:56.736 align:middle
from full maximum employment.

21:57.386 --> 22:00.056 align:middle
But we have high inflation, so
it's kind of the opposite case.

22:00.056 --> 22:03.226 align:middle
And we have to-we have
to deal with that.

22:03.226 --> 22:05.726 align:middle
Any central bank has to
deal with that by looking

22:05.726 --> 22:09.576 align:middle
at the inflation and asking
whether it is broad based

22:09.646 --> 22:12.616 align:middle
and likely to be
persistent and, and,

22:12.816 --> 22:15.236 align:middle
and whether inflation
expectations are implicated

22:15.236 --> 22:17.486 align:middle
in a way that, that
could cause them to rise.

22:17.896 --> 22:22.066 align:middle
So we're monitoring that very
carefully, and we're prepared

22:22.066 --> 22:23.486 align:middle
to use our tools as appropriate.

22:23.486 --> 22:26.306 align:middle
But, again, I think our-I
think our framework is pretty

22:26.306 --> 22:26.966 align:middle
well understood.

22:26.966 --> 22:30.436 align:middle
And, and I think the real test
of it will be down the road

22:30.436 --> 22:33.036 align:middle
when it's time to think about
raising interest rates and,

22:33.036 --> 22:36.486 align:middle
and how we assess,
assess that set of issues.

22:36.486 --> 22:37.126 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

22:39.146 --> 22:39.546 align:middle
Thank you.

22:39.546 --> 22:40.746 align:middle
We'll go to Rich at Bloomberg.

22:40.746 --> 22:40.996 align:middle
RICH MILLER.

22:40.996 --> 22:43.656 align:middle
Thanks very much, Michelle.

22:44.266 --> 22:47.216 align:middle
Chairman Powell, you, you
just alluded to the fact

22:47.216 --> 22:50.146 align:middle
that you're prepared to use
your tools to slow the economy

22:50.146 --> 22:53.366 align:middle
down if need be to-if
inflation looks

22:53.366 --> 22:54.386 align:middle
like it's getting
out of control.

22:54.386 --> 22:56.406 align:middle
I want to just try to
understand that in the context

22:56.406 --> 22:58.476 align:middle
of the framework and
the forward guidance.

22:58.476 --> 23:03.246 align:middle
Does that mean you'd be prepared
to raise interest rates even

23:03.246 --> 23:05.986 align:middle
if we're not at maximum
employment at that point,

23:06.226 --> 23:08.236 align:middle
should you see this
danger of inflation?

23:08.236 --> 23:12.206 align:middle
And, and, two, does it
also mean you'd be prepared

23:12.206 --> 23:17.756 align:middle
to raise interest rates even
if you are still buying assets?

23:17.756 --> 23:18.086 align:middle
Thank you.

23:18.916 --> 23:19.396 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:19.476 --> 23:22.256 align:middle
So there's a-there's a
part in our Statement

23:22.256 --> 23:25.476 align:middle
on Longer-Run Goals and, and
Monetary Policy Strategy, which,

23:25.756 --> 23:28.416 align:middle
Rich, I'm sure-I'm sure
you're familiar with, which,

23:28.416 --> 23:29.866 align:middle
which talks about that case

23:29.866 --> 23:32.466 align:middle
in which the two
goals are in tension.

23:32.536 --> 23:35.916 align:middle
Most of the time, if
you have high inflation,

23:35.916 --> 23:38.476 align:middle
you also have high
employment-they,

23:38.476 --> 23:39.496 align:middle
they tend to go together.

23:40.086 --> 23:43.436 align:middle
This is a situation
where they're temporarily

23:43.436 --> 23:44.416 align:middle
in different directions.

23:44.416 --> 23:45.686 align:middle
We're not at full employment,

23:45.686 --> 23:47.836 align:middle
but we are having
high inflation.

23:48.276 --> 23:51.796 align:middle
We feel like we're going
to be making good progress

23:51.836 --> 23:55.656 align:middle
over the next-over the course of
the next year, couple of years,

23:55.656 --> 23:57.336 align:middle
really, toward maximum
employment.

23:57.336 --> 23:59.086 align:middle
This is a very strong
labor market.

23:59.086 --> 24:02.966 align:middle
If you look at the number of job
openings compared to the number

24:02.966 --> 24:05.926 align:middle
of unemployed, it's-we're,
we're clearly on a path

24:06.166 --> 24:10.516 align:middle
to a very strong labor market
with high participation,

24:10.856 --> 24:13.376 align:middle
low unemployment, high
employment, wages moving

24:13.376 --> 24:15.386 align:middle
up across the spectrum.

24:15.386 --> 24:17.346 align:middle
That, that's, that's the
path that we're on, and,

24:17.346 --> 24:19.086 align:middle
and it shouldn't take that long

24:19.456 --> 24:21.426 align:middle
in macroeconomic
time to get there.

24:22.106 --> 24:26.146 align:middle
So that's, that's what I think
is, is really the likely case.

24:26.216 --> 24:30.396 align:middle
And, again, it's
not-it's not timely for us

24:30.396 --> 24:31.546 align:middle
to be thinking about,

24:31.546 --> 24:33.706 align:middle
about raising interest
rates right now.

24:33.706 --> 24:35.216 align:middle
What we're doing is,
we're, we're looking

24:35.216 --> 24:38.266 align:middle
at our asset purchases
and judging what is right

24:38.266 --> 24:43.126 align:middle
for the economy and judging
how we-how close we are

24:43.126 --> 24:45.146 align:middle
to substantial further progress

24:45.256 --> 24:47.136 align:middle
and then-and then
tapering after that.

24:47.836 --> 24:49.966 align:middle
The question you asked
about, would we raise rates

24:49.966 --> 24:52.816 align:middle
if we hadn't finished
cutting-hadn't finished the

24:52.876 --> 24:54.896 align:middle
taper, hypothetical question.

24:54.896 --> 24:57.616 align:middle
You know, we'd, we'd face
the circumstance at the time.

24:58.186 --> 25:01.066 align:middle
We could always just-you know,
one thing one could do would be

25:01.066 --> 25:04.386 align:middle
to just cut asset
purchases all the way to zero

25:04.386 --> 25:05.226 align:middle
if you wanted to do that.

25:05.596 --> 25:07.286 align:middle
But it's, it's, it's
just hard to-it's hard

25:07.286 --> 25:08.306 align:middle
to answer what you would do

25:08.306 --> 25:10.126 align:middle
without knowing a lot
more about the situation.

25:10.376 --> 25:12.566 align:middle
Ideally, you wouldn't
be still buying assets

25:12.566 --> 25:13.896 align:middle
and raising rates
because, of course,

25:13.896 --> 25:16.646 align:middle
you're adding accommodation
by buying and,

25:16.646 --> 25:18.596 align:middle
and removing accommodation
by raising rates.

25:18.596 --> 25:19.936 align:middle
So that wouldn't be ideal.

25:20.736 --> 25:21.296 align:middle
I'll say that.

25:21.336 --> 25:21.906 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

25:24.706 --> 25:25.076 align:middle
Thank you.

25:25.076 --> 25:27.096 align:middle
We'll go to Edward
Lawrence at Fox Business.

25:27.096 --> 25:27.526 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

25:28.836 --> 25:29.966 align:middle
So thank you, Mr. Chairman.

25:29.966 --> 25:31.336 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman,
for taking the question.

25:31.336 --> 25:32.326 align:middle
Now, so you're talking
about jobs.

25:32.326 --> 25:35.106 align:middle
You just said we're on a path
to a very strong job market.

25:35.106 --> 25:38.056 align:middle
You know, we have 9.5
million people unemployed

25:38.056 --> 25:40.366 align:middle
as of the last labor
market survey

25:40.366 --> 25:42.356 align:middle
and 9.2 million job openings.

25:42.736 --> 25:44.596 align:middle
So what's the-what's
the disconnect?

25:44.596 --> 25:45.636 align:middle
Is it a skills gap?

25:45.636 --> 25:47.696 align:middle
Is it the extended
unemployment benefits?

25:47.696 --> 25:50.126 align:middle
Is it the fact that people just
aren't willing to relocate?

25:50.126 --> 25:51.586 align:middle
What is the disconnect there?

25:53.106 --> 25:53.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:53.516 --> 25:55.976 align:middle
It's a-it's a really
unusual situation

25:55.976 --> 25:59.526 align:middle
to have-to have the
ratio of vacancies

25:59.526 --> 26:01.956 align:middle
to unemployed be this high.

26:02.666 --> 26:04.626 align:middle
And we think there are a
number of things at work there.

26:06.176 --> 26:11.086 align:middle
Maybe the place to start is, is
just to say that this is now not

26:11.086 --> 26:13.516 align:middle
so much about people going
back to their old jobs.

26:13.516 --> 26:14.836 align:middle
It's about finding a new job.

26:14.836 --> 26:19.206 align:middle
So that's a time-intensive,
labor-intensive process.

26:19.816 --> 26:22.976 align:middle
And there may be a bit
of a speed limit on that.

26:22.976 --> 26:24.456 align:middle
There, there's research
that suggests

26:24.456 --> 26:26.036 align:middle
that there is a speed
limit on that.

26:26.036 --> 26:29.106 align:middle
So it's not like you can
have millions of people

26:29.106 --> 26:31.466 align:middle
at the beginning of the recovery
going back in a single month

26:31.506 --> 26:33.366 align:middle
because they're just going
back to their old job.

26:33.366 --> 26:35.286 align:middle
This is about job
selection, things like that.

26:35.836 --> 26:37.666 align:middle
There may also be some factors

26:37.666 --> 26:39.586 align:middle
that are-that are
holding people back,

26:39.586 --> 26:41.246 align:middle
and that's-this is
what surveys say.

26:41.246 --> 26:44.316 align:middle
There are people who, who are
reluctant to go back to work

26:44.316 --> 26:46.926 align:middle
because they still
feel exposed to COVID.

26:47.466 --> 26:50.456 align:middle
These could be jobs where,
where there was a lot of,

26:50.456 --> 26:52.026 align:middle
of interaction with the public

26:52.616 --> 26:55.436 align:middle
and where perhaps there's a
family member who's vulnerable

26:55.436 --> 26:57.086 align:middle
or for whatever reason.

26:57.556 --> 27:00.086 align:middle
There's also caretakers
who are-where,

27:00.086 --> 27:03.636 align:middle
where schools are not fully
open and parents are at home

27:04.326 --> 27:06.816 align:middle
or taking care of,
of, of older people.

27:07.296 --> 27:11.606 align:middle
And there's also been very
generous unemployment benefits,

27:11.606 --> 27:12.826 align:middle
which are now rolling off.

27:12.826 --> 27:15.226 align:middle
They'll be-they'll
be fully rolled off

27:15.226 --> 27:16.116 align:middle
in a couple of months.

27:16.506 --> 27:19.376 align:middle
And all of those factors
should, should wane.

27:20.166 --> 27:23.346 align:middle
And, you know, we, we think we
should see, because of that,

27:23.346 --> 27:27.116 align:middle
we should see strong job
creation moving forward.

27:27.166 --> 27:30.366 align:middle
I mean, ultimately, it's
unusual to see aspects

27:30.366 --> 27:33.426 align:middle
like the job openings number
in a context where there are

27:33.426 --> 27:34.626 align:middle
that many unemployed people.

27:34.626 --> 27:37.286 align:middle
That many job openings would
typically suggest a tight

27:37.286 --> 27:37.826 align:middle
labor market.

27:37.826 --> 27:41.136 align:middle
And, of course, we hear from
businesses all over the country

27:41.656 --> 27:43.066 align:middle
that it's very hard
to hire people.

27:43.576 --> 27:45.856 align:middle
And that may be because people,

27:46.236 --> 27:49.086 align:middle
people are shopping
carefully for their next job.

27:49.476 --> 27:51.906 align:middle
I mean, I, I, I think
the bottom line

27:51.906 --> 27:53.346 align:middle
on this is, people want to work.

27:53.706 --> 27:57.006 align:middle
If you look at where labor
force participation can get,

27:57.096 --> 28:00.116 align:middle
people will go back to
work unless they retire.

28:00.116 --> 28:01.086 align:middle
Some people will retire.

28:01.086 --> 28:04.606 align:middle
But, generally speaking,
Americans want to work, and,

28:04.606 --> 28:06.976 align:middle
and they'll find their way
into the jobs that they want.

28:07.746 --> 28:08.846 align:middle
It may take some time, though.

28:08.846 --> 28:09.486 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

28:12.746 --> 28:13.566 align:middle
Thank you.

28:13.756 --> 28:15.466 align:middle
Rachel Siegel at
the Washington Post.

28:15.466 --> 28:16.556 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

28:16.556 --> 28:17.476 align:middle
Thank you, Michelle.

28:17.476 --> 28:20.416 align:middle
And thank you, Chair Powell,
for taking our questions.

28:20.416 --> 28:23.576 align:middle
I wanted to follow up on what
you said about there tending

28:23.576 --> 28:25.976 align:middle
to be less in the way
of economic implications

28:25.976 --> 28:27.966 align:middle
from each wave of COVID cases.

28:28.096 --> 28:30.226 align:middle
Could you elaborate
on what you've seen

28:30.226 --> 28:32.616 align:middle
to that end during
previous waves?

28:32.616 --> 28:34.236 align:middle
And then, looking forward,

28:34.236 --> 28:38.256 align:middle
how vulnerable is the labor
market to the Delta variant?

28:38.256 --> 28:41.586 align:middle
I'm thinking or wondering if
you have concerns that jobs

28:41.586 --> 28:42.836 align:middle
that may have come
back in travel

28:42.836 --> 28:45.486 align:middle
or tourism could be susceptible

28:45.486 --> 28:48.466 align:middle
if people started
reconsidering their travel plans

28:48.576 --> 28:50.896 align:middle
or if caretakers were suddenly
faced with the prospect

28:50.896 --> 28:52.276 align:middle
of schools not reopening.

28:52.276 --> 28:54.706 align:middle
How do you see some of
those risks going forward?

28:54.706 --> 28:55.536 align:middle
Thank you so much.

28:56.166 --> 28:56.856 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:57.016 --> 28:59.756 align:middle
Sure. So if you remember the,
the summer wave last year

29:00.446 --> 29:04.206 align:middle
of COVID, which was largely
southern and western states,

29:04.956 --> 29:08.716 align:middle
the economy just performed much
better than anyone expected.

29:08.806 --> 29:10.736 align:middle
You know, we, we were
coming off the spring wave,

29:10.736 --> 29:11.956 align:middle
where there were a
lot of shutdowns.

29:11.956 --> 29:13.666 align:middle
And then this big
second wave hit,

29:14.336 --> 29:17.206 align:middle
and I think the natural thing
to do is to expect that,

29:17.266 --> 29:19.886 align:middle
that it would have a real
impact on the economy.

29:19.886 --> 29:22.386 align:middle
And it was much less
than, than people thought.

29:22.836 --> 29:24.966 align:middle
People-what's happened
is, first of all,

29:24.966 --> 29:26.496 align:middle
many people are vaccinated.

29:26.496 --> 29:27.496 align:middle
They're going on
with their lives.

29:27.496 --> 29:29.086 align:middle
Secondly, we've kind of
learned to live with it.

29:29.086 --> 29:33.166 align:middle
A lot of industries have, have
kind of improvised their way

29:33.166 --> 29:35.966 align:middle
around it-particularly, for
example, buying a new home.

29:35.966 --> 29:38.966 align:middle
That, that process of buying
a new home very quickly moved

29:38.966 --> 29:40.826 align:middle
to much more of a
virtual process.

29:41.276 --> 29:43.406 align:middle
And so they were able to,
you know, to, to do that.

29:43.406 --> 29:46.386 align:middle
And other industries as well
have gone to take-out and,

29:46.386 --> 29:47.896 align:middle
you know, no-contact things.

29:47.896 --> 29:50.656 align:middle
It's-so that can all-it seems

29:50.656 --> 29:53.086 align:middle
like we've learned
to, to handle this.

29:53.086 --> 29:55.916 align:middle
Now, I think people would
like to get back to, to,

29:56.456 --> 29:57.886 align:middle
you know, the way things were.

29:58.306 --> 30:01.496 align:middle
And I, I, I hope to some
extent, we will over time.

30:01.566 --> 30:04.686 align:middle
You know, of course, the big
wave we had last winter did have

30:04.686 --> 30:08.826 align:middle
significant employment effects,
particularly in, you know,

30:08.826 --> 30:11.996 align:middle
hospitality and leisure and
other entertainment-other,

30:11.996 --> 30:14.566 align:middle
other areas with a
lot of direct contact.

30:14.566 --> 30:15.886 align:middle
A lot of jobs were lost then

30:15.886 --> 30:19.196 align:middle
because that was a very strong
wave that happened in, in,

30:19.196 --> 30:20.806 align:middle
in the winter months last year,

30:20.806 --> 30:22.286 align:middle
just before the vaccines
arrived.

30:22.856 --> 30:25.106 align:middle
So with Delta, we're just
going to have to watch.

30:25.106 --> 30:27.906 align:middle
Again, with, with, with a
reasonably high percentage

30:27.906 --> 30:29.426 align:middle
of the country vaccinated

30:29.926 --> 30:33.506 align:middle
and the vaccine apparently being
effective-we're not experts

30:33.506 --> 30:34.846 align:middle
on this-but it, it seems

30:34.846 --> 30:39.516 align:middle
like the-a good-a good
going-in estimate would be

30:39.516 --> 30:42.236 align:middle
that the effects will
probably be less.

30:42.726 --> 30:44.846 align:middle
There probably won't be
significant lockdowns

30:44.846 --> 30:45.516 align:middle
and things like that.

30:45.516 --> 30:48.096 align:middle
But, again, those, those are
not decisions for us or, or,

30:48.236 --> 30:51.066 align:middle
or-nor is it something
we're be-we'd be expert in.

30:51.386 --> 30:53.716 align:middle
In terms of the, the,
the channels, you know,

30:53.716 --> 30:55.806 align:middle
I-this is-this is
kind of speculation,

30:55.806 --> 31:00.206 align:middle
but it's pretty-it
just is that people,

31:00.316 --> 31:02.106 align:middle
you could imagine
school districts deciding

31:02.106 --> 31:03.796 align:middle
to wait a month or two

31:04.476 --> 31:06.706 align:middle
for the-for the-for
the Delta wave to go.

31:07.326 --> 31:08.646 align:middle
I'm not saying this'll happen,

31:08.646 --> 31:10.046 align:middle
but that it's easy
to imagine that.

31:10.046 --> 31:11.536 align:middle
It's also easy to imagine

31:11.536 --> 31:14.876 align:middle
that some people might
say, you know what?

31:14.876 --> 31:16.556 align:middle
I'm just going to-I'm just
going to wait a couple

31:16.556 --> 31:17.906 align:middle
of months before
going back to work.

31:18.086 --> 31:19.646 align:middle
Wouldn't be hard to
imagine that happening.

31:20.326 --> 31:23.736 align:middle
If, if schools don't open, then
caretakers have to stay home.

31:23.736 --> 31:26.496 align:middle
And if people don't go
back into the labor force,

31:26.496 --> 31:28.866 align:middle
then the job growth won't be as
strong, those kinds of things.

31:28.866 --> 31:32.106 align:middle
So I don't-it doesn't-it
doesn't-again,

31:32.106 --> 31:34.166 align:middle
sitting here today,
not being able

31:34.166 --> 31:35.946 align:middle
to really know the
future-it doesn't seem

31:35.946 --> 31:37.626 align:middle
as though the effects
will be very large.

31:37.626 --> 31:39.086 align:middle
But there may be effects.

31:39.126 --> 31:42.646 align:middle
And they-it may be that the
effect is to slow the economy

31:42.646 --> 31:45.056 align:middle
down just for a period
of months-or not.

31:45.126 --> 31:46.376 align:middle
There are many parts
of the country

31:46.376 --> 31:49.696 align:middle
where it might not have an
effect, and we're just going

31:49.696 --> 31:51.636 align:middle
to have to, to see what
the economic effects are.

31:51.826 --> 31:52.326 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:54.376 --> 31:55.036 align:middle
Thank you.

31:55.036 --> 31:56.816 align:middle
Now we'll go to David
Gura at NPR.

31:56.816 --> 31:57.826 align:middle
DAVID GURA.

31:59.006 --> 32:00.026 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

32:00.026 --> 32:02.226 align:middle
I was going to ask if you've
started drafting your Jackson

32:02.226 --> 32:03.456 align:middle
Hole speech, but I'm going to go

32:03.456 --> 32:04.786 align:middle
in a-in a different
direction here.

32:05.246 --> 32:07.176 align:middle
Steve Liesman asked
you about semantics.

32:07.176 --> 32:10.326 align:middle
I'm going to take a cue from
him and ask you about the term

32:10.326 --> 32:13.226 align:middle
"transitory," because I think
of you as somebody who's

32:13.226 --> 32:16.956 align:middle
so lucidly explained policies
and programs in recent months

32:16.956 --> 32:19.696 align:middle
and made a real concerted
effort to explain them to,

32:19.696 --> 32:21.896 align:middle
to the American public
as a whole.

32:21.896 --> 32:23.946 align:middle
I wonder about this term and
what you would say to people

32:23.946 --> 32:26.396 align:middle
who don't know it
definitionally,

32:26.506 --> 32:28.716 align:middle
don't know what it means,
and see prices going up

32:28.716 --> 32:31.036 align:middle
and wonder how long they're
going to have to wait.

32:31.036 --> 32:34.506 align:middle
So just some broader definition
from you-I'd like to hear just

32:34.506 --> 32:36.536 align:middle
about what it means or
how you understand it.

32:36.536 --> 32:39.676 align:middle
And, and quickly as well, you've
talked about vaccines a bit.

32:39.676 --> 32:42.856 align:middle
And your colleague in
Minneapolis has placed a mandate

32:42.856 --> 32:44.536 align:middle
on vaccinating employees
coming back

32:44.536 --> 32:45.586 align:middle
to that Federal Reserve Bank.

32:45.586 --> 32:48.446 align:middle
And I wonder if that's something
that you would like to see

32:48.446 --> 32:50.446 align:middle
or expect to see
Systemwide going forward.

32:51.096 --> 32:51.336 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:51.576 --> 32:53.816 align:middle
The concept of "transitory"
is really this:

32:54.356 --> 32:59.056 align:middle
It is that the increases
will happen.

32:59.746 --> 33:01.236 align:middle
We're not saying
they will reverse.

33:01.296 --> 33:02.606 align:middle
That's not what "transitory"
means.

33:02.606 --> 33:05.186 align:middle
It means that the increases
in prices will happen,

33:05.476 --> 33:09.226 align:middle
so there will be inflation
but that the process

33:09.226 --> 33:13.376 align:middle
of inflation will
stop so that-so

33:13.376 --> 33:15.236 align:middle
that there won't be
further-when, when we think

33:15.236 --> 33:17.086 align:middle
of inflation, we really
think of inflation going

33:17.086 --> 33:19.146 align:middle
up year upon year
upon year upon year.

33:19.676 --> 33:20.556 align:middle
That's inflation.

33:21.236 --> 33:23.266 align:middle
When you have inflation
for 12 months

33:23.266 --> 33:25.296 align:middle
or whatever it might be-I'm
just taking an example;

33:25.296 --> 33:28.676 align:middle
I'm not making an estimate-then,
then you have a price increase,

33:29.196 --> 33:31.126 align:middle
but you don't have
an inflation process.

33:31.596 --> 33:35.066 align:middle
And so part of that
just is that,

33:35.216 --> 33:38.396 align:middle
if it doesn't affect longer-term
inflation expectations,

33:38.806 --> 33:44.366 align:middle
then it's very likely not to
infect-to affect the process

33:44.366 --> 33:45.706 align:middle
of inflation going forward.

33:45.786 --> 33:48.266 align:middle
So what, what I mean by
"transitory" is just something

33:48.266 --> 33:52.056 align:middle
that doesn't leave a permanent
mark on the inflation process.

33:52.666 --> 33:55.306 align:middle
Again, we don't mean-I don't
mean that, that, that, that,

33:55.556 --> 33:58.836 align:middle
you know, producers are going to
take those price increases back.

33:58.836 --> 33:59.836 align:middle
That's, that's not the idea.

33:59.836 --> 34:01.596 align:middle
It's just that they
won't go on indefinitely.

34:02.036 --> 34:04.426 align:middle
So to the extent
people are, are,

34:04.466 --> 34:08.396 align:middle
are implementing price increases
because raw materials are going

34:08.396 --> 34:12.566 align:middle
up or labor costs or
something's going up, you know,

34:12.566 --> 34:15.016 align:middle
the question, really,
for inflation really is,

34:15.106 --> 34:16.416 align:middle
does that mean they're
going to go

34:16.416 --> 34:18.396 align:middle
up the next year
by the same amount?

34:18.396 --> 34:21.136 align:middle
So you're going to be in
a process where inflation,

34:21.136 --> 34:22.726 align:middle
the inflation process,
gets going.

34:22.726 --> 34:25.246 align:middle
And, and that happens
because people's expectations

34:25.526 --> 34:28.496 align:middle
about future inflation move up.

34:28.496 --> 34:29.666 align:middle
And we don't think
that's happening.

34:29.666 --> 34:31.046 align:middle
There's no evidence
that it's happening.

34:31.046 --> 34:32.966 align:middle
All the evidence is
that it's not happening.

34:33.626 --> 34:36.366 align:middle
But, nonetheless, we have
to watch this very carefully

34:36.366 --> 34:39.066 align:middle
because this is-you know,
we have two mandates:

34:39.066 --> 34:40.636 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability.

34:40.666 --> 34:41.676 align:middle
Price stability, for us,

34:41.676 --> 34:44.336 align:middle
means inflation averaging
2 percent over time.

34:44.336 --> 34:46.856 align:middle
And so we've got to be
very careful about that.

34:46.896 --> 34:49.306 align:middle
But, but I, I, I think it's
a good point that it's,

34:49.806 --> 34:51.866 align:middle
it's a term-what it
really means is temporary.

34:52.776 --> 34:55.036 align:middle
But then you've got to
understand that it doesn't mean

34:55.036 --> 34:56.946 align:middle
that the-that the
increases will be taken back.

34:56.946 --> 35:00.726 align:middle
Some of them will be, but, but
that's not really what it means.

35:00.726 --> 35:04.256 align:middle
In terms of-so we're, we're
working virtually here,

35:04.766 --> 35:09.286 align:middle
and we'll be coming back down
the road in a couple of months,

35:09.286 --> 35:11.446 align:middle
starting to bring people
back here at the Board

35:11.446 --> 35:12.686 align:middle
of Governors in Washington.

35:13.296 --> 35:14.546 align:middle
And, you know, we're going

35:14.546 --> 35:16.906 align:middle
to follow public health
guidance and things like that.

35:16.906 --> 35:20.046 align:middle
We really haven't made
the fundamental decisions

35:20.046 --> 35:21.696 align:middle
about exactly what
that will look like.

35:21.696 --> 35:23.976 align:middle
And it'll depend on, to some
extent, on what-on what,

35:24.646 --> 35:26.426 align:middle
you know, CDC guidance looks

35:26.426 --> 35:29.346 align:middle
like when we actually
do bring people back in.

35:29.706 --> 35:29.986 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

35:30.756 --> 35:32.516 align:middle
Thank you.

35:32.686 --> 35:34.766 align:middle
We'll go to Michael
McKee at Bloomberg TV.

35:34.766 --> 35:36.246 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

35:36.396 --> 35:37.666 align:middle
Chairman, I wanted to
ask you a little bit

35:37.666 --> 35:40.986 align:middle
about your taper
timeline in the sense

35:40.986 --> 35:44.406 align:middle
that you said you want a
couple more months of data.

35:44.406 --> 35:48.896 align:middle
And the statement says that
the Fed is going to use-to,

35:48.896 --> 35:53.956 align:middle
to evaluate the developments
in the two markets, in jobs

35:53.956 --> 35:56.226 align:middle
and inflation, in
coming meetings.

35:56.466 --> 35:59.726 align:middle
Does that suggest that we
wouldn't see anything before

35:59.726 --> 36:02.386 align:middle
September or November
in your meetings?

36:02.386 --> 36:03.526 align:middle
And I know a lot of people

36:03.526 --> 36:07.566 align:middle
on Wall Street have basically
felt you're going to lay

36:07.566 --> 36:10.006 align:middle
out your taper plans
at Jackson Hole.

36:10.006 --> 36:13.486 align:middle
Is that-is that the
plan, or are we not going

36:13.486 --> 36:14.616 align:middle
to see anything until the fall?

36:14.816 --> 36:15.106 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:15.106 --> 36:17.166 align:middle
So in any decisions
about the timing,

36:17.166 --> 36:19.816 align:middle
and I did not-if I-if
I said we're looking

36:19.816 --> 36:21.546 align:middle
at wanting a couple
more months of data,

36:21.546 --> 36:23.516 align:middle
I wasn't-I'm not meaning
to suggest anything

36:23.516 --> 36:26.756 align:middle
about a particular time
at which we might taper,

36:26.756 --> 36:28.656 align:middle
because we really have
not made that decision.

36:29.316 --> 36:32.846 align:middle
All I'm saying is, is, we're not
at substantial further progress.

36:33.516 --> 36:37.726 align:middle
There's a range of views on, on
what timing will be appropriate.

36:37.726 --> 36:39.206 align:middle
And those views ultimately
track,

36:39.306 --> 36:42.366 align:middle
track back to people's
views about the economy and,

36:42.366 --> 36:45.316 align:middle
and what will happen as
we make progress towards,

36:45.416 --> 36:46.776 align:middle
towards our-towards our goal.

36:46.776 --> 36:48.666 align:middle
So that's, that's
really what it is.

36:48.666 --> 36:51.606 align:middle
And I, I-we will, of
course, as we-you know,

36:51.606 --> 36:53.996 align:middle
we're going to continue
to try to provide clarity

36:53.996 --> 37:00.366 align:middle
as appropriate on, on
timing, pace, and composition.

37:00.366 --> 37:04.806 align:middle
But, today, I've, I've given
you what I can give you because,

37:04.806 --> 37:06.966 align:middle
again, this, this was the
first, really, I would say,

37:06.966 --> 37:11.856 align:middle
deep dive on the issues of
timing, pace, and composition.

37:12.306 --> 37:14.096 align:middle
And it was a good meeting.

37:14.096 --> 37:16.686 align:middle
And-but no decisions are
made, and, and I'm just not

37:16.686 --> 37:18.636 align:middle
in a position to, to
give you much guidance,

37:18.636 --> 37:21.866 align:middle
really any guidance,
on the actual timing.

37:22.066 --> 37:24.496 align:middle
I-but I will say
we're making progress.

37:24.836 --> 37:26.226 align:middle
We expect further progress.

37:26.886 --> 37:30.476 align:middle
And we expect that,
if, if things go well,

37:30.476 --> 37:32.106 align:middle
then we will-we will
reach that goal.

37:32.106 --> 37:34.396 align:middle
And when we reach it and
the Committee is comfortable

37:34.746 --> 37:38.126 align:middle
that we have reached it, then,
then we'll taper at that point.

37:38.366 --> 37:40.496 align:middle
I can't, I really-there's
nothing I can say

37:40.496 --> 37:41.526 align:middle
about Jackson Hole.

37:41.806 --> 37:44.426 align:middle
You know, I'm in-we're in the
process of writing that speech.

37:44.426 --> 37:47.536 align:middle
And I am going to give
a speech, and-but I, I,

37:47.786 --> 37:50.856 align:middle
I wouldn't want to-I wouldn't
want to say what will be

37:50.856 --> 37:51.666 align:middle
in there at this point.

37:52.026 --> 37:53.506 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

37:53.506 --> 37:54.966 align:middle
Well, if I could follow up,

37:55.076 --> 37:57.266 align:middle
several people have
recently noted

37:57.266 --> 38:00.556 align:middle
that the Fed has got the
markets working well.

38:00.556 --> 38:03.146 align:middle
And you've got bank loans up.

38:03.146 --> 38:05.096 align:middle
In other words, you've
stimulated demand.

38:05.526 --> 38:08.866 align:middle
But savers and companies,
like insurance companies

38:08.866 --> 38:12.556 align:middle
and pension funds, are getting
hammered by the low rates.

38:12.556 --> 38:15.056 align:middle
And they're wondering if
the balance hasn't started

38:15.056 --> 38:17.736 align:middle
to shift away from
benefiting the economy

38:18.066 --> 38:19.696 align:middle
to doing more harm than good.

38:20.566 --> 38:21.686 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:21.776 --> 38:25.326 align:middle
We-asset purchases were just
a key part of our response

38:25.326 --> 38:27.016 align:middle
to the critical phase
of the crisis.

38:27.536 --> 38:31.036 align:middle
They, they really helped
us restore market function

38:31.036 --> 38:35.706 align:middle
to these key markets, really, on
which-on which, which are very,

38:35.706 --> 38:37.866 align:middle
very important to our economy
and the global economy.

38:38.216 --> 38:40.756 align:middle
And then they were
a big part of,

38:40.756 --> 38:42.726 align:middle
of creating accommodative
financial conditions

38:42.726 --> 38:43.706 align:middle
to support demand.

38:44.076 --> 38:45.496 align:middle
They were strongly needed.

38:45.496 --> 38:48.876 align:middle
It was that commitment to
continue asset purchases

38:48.876 --> 38:52.556 align:middle
that provided strong
support for the economy and,

38:52.556 --> 38:54.756 align:middle
and has been a part of the story

38:54.756 --> 38:56.526 align:middle
for why the economy is
so strong right now.

38:56.526 --> 38:58.896 align:middle
So we said we would taper

38:58.896 --> 39:01.256 align:middle
when we-when we achieve
substantial further progress.

39:01.256 --> 39:03.466 align:middle
And, and, you know, we're
going to honor that commitment.

39:03.466 --> 39:07.116 align:middle
I mean, it's-and, and, again,
we're talking about it right now

39:07.376 --> 39:10.926 align:middle
and meeting by meeting and,
and moving in that direction.

39:10.926 --> 39:12.686 align:middle
We will taper when
we reach that goal.

39:13.116 --> 39:16.816 align:middle
And we'll provide more, you
know, more clarity on that

39:16.816 --> 39:18.586 align:middle
as we go, as is appropriate.

39:18.586 --> 39:19.226 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:21.516 --> 39:21.926 align:middle
Thank you.

39:21.926 --> 39:23.996 align:middle
We'll go to Hannah Lang
at the American Banker.

39:25.756 --> 39:27.466 align:middle
HANNAH LANG.

39:27.466 --> 39:29.776 align:middle
Hi. You've been asked
a lot previously

39:29.776 --> 39:33.456 align:middle
if the Fed's monetary policy at
all contributes to inequality,

39:33.456 --> 39:35.276 align:middle
but I was really curious to know

39:35.276 --> 39:38.156 align:middle
where you think the Fed's
regulatory policy lands,

39:38.156 --> 39:39.696 align:middle
particularly if you think

39:39.696 --> 39:42.626 align:middle
that the Fed's bank capital
requirements have had the effect

39:42.626 --> 39:45.506 align:middle
of penalizing lower-income
households seeking loans.

39:45.956 --> 39:48.336 align:middle
Basically, is it possible
to achieve the balance

39:48.336 --> 39:50.116 align:middle
of a safe banking
system with one

39:50.116 --> 39:52.896 align:middle
that also provides
equitable loan access?

39:52.936 --> 39:55.606 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

39:55.786 --> 39:59.846 align:middle
Well, I think strong capital
requirements are essential

39:59.956 --> 40:02.046 align:middle
for banks, particularly
for the largest banks.

40:02.686 --> 40:06.326 align:middle
And I think that an
undercapitalized banking system,

40:06.326 --> 40:09.986 align:middle
as we've seen, can be a
real threat to the economy

40:09.986 --> 40:14.586 align:middle
and most-mostly, or to,
to the greatest extent,

40:14.586 --> 40:16.596 align:middle
to people at the lower end
of the income spectrum.

40:17.176 --> 40:21.296 align:middle
So if you look back at not
this previous-not this crisis

40:21.296 --> 40:22.306 align:middle
but the previous one,

40:22.836 --> 40:25.146 align:middle
the bank-the banking
system was undercapitalized.

40:25.146 --> 40:29.976 align:middle
So higher capital requirements
are, are, you know, are,

40:29.976 --> 40:33.386 align:middle
are really a good thing
because they allow banks

40:33.386 --> 40:35.636 align:middle
to weather downturns
and continue

40:35.636 --> 40:37.226 align:middle
to perform the functions
that they perform.

40:38.166 --> 40:43.676 align:middle
I think it's other tools that
we have to-and the Fed has some

40:43.676 --> 40:45.736 align:middle
of these tools, Congress
has some of these tools,

40:45.736 --> 40:48.666 align:middle
other agencies have some
of these tools-to assure

40:48.876 --> 40:53.076 align:middle
or support the wide availability
of credit, particularly to low-

40:53.076 --> 40:54.326 align:middle
and moderate-income communities.

40:54.326 --> 40:55.976 align:middle
So that's CRA.

40:56.076 --> 40:57.096 align:middle
We enforce CRA.

40:57.096 --> 41:00.656 align:middle
We're working on a-on a
new CRA proposal right now

41:00.656 --> 41:03.066 align:middle
with the other banking agencies,
and we think it's going

41:03.066 --> 41:06.726 align:middle
to be-it's going to be good and
will support the flow of credit

41:06.726 --> 41:09.156 align:middle
to low- and moderate-income
households.

41:09.546 --> 41:12.626 align:middle
It's also the anti-anti-lending
discrimination statutes

41:12.626 --> 41:13.626 align:middle
that we enforce.

41:14.676 --> 41:17.366 align:middle
And it's some of the programs
that Congress has in place

41:17.366 --> 41:18.756 align:middle
to support the flow
of credit to low-

41:18.756 --> 41:19.916 align:middle
and moderate-income communities.

41:19.976 --> 41:21.696 align:middle
I, I don't think it's
capital standards at all.

41:21.696 --> 41:23.426 align:middle
I think capital standards
work the other way.

41:23.466 --> 41:26.246 align:middle
Strong capital is what
enables banks to continue

41:26.246 --> 41:28.076 align:middle
to serve their communities,
including low-

41:28.076 --> 41:29.166 align:middle
and moderate-income communities.

41:29.166 --> 41:30.396 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:30.736 --> 41:32.276 align:middle
Thank you.

41:32.276 --> 41:34.816 align:middle
We'll go to Chris
Rugaber at the AP.

41:36.206 --> 41:37.136 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

41:37.136 --> 41:40.476 align:middle
Hi. Thank you for
taking my question.

41:40.476 --> 41:42.146 align:middle
Chair Powell, I guess
I wanted to ask

41:42.146 --> 41:46.346 align:middle
about the last-when you were
before Congress earlier this

41:46.346 --> 41:49.106 align:middle
month, you mentioned, I think,
something along the lines of,

41:49.496 --> 41:52.516 align:middle
it won't take too long
before we see if you're right

41:52.516 --> 41:56.306 align:middle
about inflation and
its temporary aspect.

41:56.306 --> 41:57.786 align:middle
You also mentioned
learning a lot more

41:57.786 --> 41:59.336 align:middle
in the next six months
about the economy.

41:59.336 --> 42:01.636 align:middle
Can you tell us a little more
of what you mean by that?

42:02.026 --> 42:04.786 align:middle
When do you think you'll get
a clean reading on inflation

42:04.786 --> 42:08.486 align:middle
that is free of most
of the distortions

42:08.486 --> 42:09.106 align:middle
that we're seeing now?

42:09.706 --> 42:10.716 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:10.906 --> 42:14.456 align:middle
Right. So with inflation,
as I mentioned, we're,

42:14.456 --> 42:16.646 align:middle
we look not just at
the headline number,

42:16.646 --> 42:18.306 align:middle
but we look at all
the components that go

42:18.306 --> 42:19.736 align:middle
into the calculation
of inflation.

42:19.736 --> 42:21.956 align:middle
And if you do that, if
you look under the hood,

42:21.956 --> 42:26.216 align:middle
what you see is not that,
widely, across the whole range

42:26.216 --> 42:29.686 align:middle
of goods and services that,
that are in the economy,

42:29.996 --> 42:32.486 align:middle
we're seeing upward,
upward pressure on prices.

42:32.486 --> 42:33.866 align:middle
That's not really
what we're seeing.

42:34.096 --> 42:36.056 align:middle
What we're seeing is
a handful of things

42:36.056 --> 42:38.956 align:middle
that are really-that
really account for the,

42:39.196 --> 42:41.936 align:middle
the overshoot of inflation.

42:41.986 --> 42:45.226 align:middle
And, as I mentioned,
it's things like cars.

42:45.276 --> 42:48.826 align:middle
You know, new, used, and rental
cars have moved up in price

42:48.826 --> 42:50.016 align:middle
because of the car shortage

42:50.016 --> 42:51.936 align:middle
because of the semiconductor
shortage.

42:52.566 --> 42:56.266 align:middle
And housing-sorry,
hotels and air,

42:56.466 --> 42:57.566 align:middle
air fares have moved back up,

42:57.566 --> 43:00.926 align:middle
but that really just is
retracing the very large

43:01.056 --> 43:03.366 align:middle
downward movement in
prices that they had before.

43:03.366 --> 43:05.556 align:middle
So that's, that's
a big, big part of,

43:06.206 --> 43:07.806 align:middle
of why the inflation
readings are so high.

43:07.806 --> 43:10.256 align:middle
And those don't-those, frankly,

43:10.886 --> 43:14.706 align:middle
don't carry significant
implications in the long run

43:14.706 --> 43:17.506 align:middle
for the-for inflation or
for the American economy.

43:18.006 --> 43:20.166 align:middle
So what I said was, we,

43:20.696 --> 43:25.136 align:middle
we're going to see whether
these things-we don't need

43:25.136 --> 43:28.746 align:middle
to see everything do what
lumber prices have done.

43:28.746 --> 43:30.376 align:middle
So if you look at lumber
prices, they went up

43:30.376 --> 43:31.126 align:middle
and then they went down.

43:31.126 --> 43:33.506 align:middle
So what we want to see
is these other things.

43:33.506 --> 43:35.506 align:middle
Do they-do they-do the
prices flatten out?

43:36.596 --> 43:37.766 align:middle
Do they actually move down?

43:37.866 --> 43:40.306 align:middle
If they flatten out, then
their, their contribution

43:40.306 --> 43:42.626 align:middle
to inflation becomes
zero over time

43:43.126 --> 43:45.006 align:middle
so that they're not
contributing to inflation.

43:45.006 --> 43:48.956 align:middle
And so, if we start to see those
things happen fairly widely

43:48.956 --> 43:52.426 align:middle
among the-among the things that
have really moved up quickly,

43:52.526 --> 43:54.986 align:middle
then we'll, we'll-they
won't all happen at once

43:54.986 --> 43:57.736 align:middle
or happen quickly-but we'll know
that our basic understanding

43:57.736 --> 44:00.076 align:middle
of the situation
is broadly correct.

44:00.376 --> 44:02.606 align:middle
And I don't think it will
take, or what I said was,

44:02.606 --> 44:05.806 align:middle
I don't think it'll
take a very long time

44:06.326 --> 44:07.726 align:middle
to see whether that's the case.

44:08.186 --> 44:11.836 align:middle
It, it, it is probably the
case that, frankly, the,

44:11.836 --> 44:14.556 align:middle
the overall reopening of
the economy is going to play

44:14.556 --> 44:16.336 align:middle
out over a period of time.

44:16.336 --> 44:19.476 align:middle
This is a historic,
world historical event,

44:19.476 --> 44:21.516 align:middle
that the global economy
is now reopening.

44:21.516 --> 44:23.606 align:middle
It's not going to-it's not
going to happen quickly.

44:23.606 --> 44:24.996 align:middle
It's going to take some time.

44:24.996 --> 44:27.326 align:middle
And it's going to be very
uneven, as we discussed before.

44:28.106 --> 44:32.116 align:middle
But I think we will-we will
know, you know, when we know.

44:32.116 --> 44:33.666 align:middle
But I don't think it
will take that long.

44:33.666 --> 44:35.206 align:middle
I don't want to put
a number on it.

44:36.016 --> 44:39.626 align:middle
But I do think that, that, if
we see those things happening,

44:39.626 --> 44:41.566 align:middle
we'll know that we have
the story basically right.

44:41.566 --> 44:42.206 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

44:44.336 --> 44:44.996 align:middle
Thank you.

44:44.996 --> 44:47.036 align:middle
We'll go to Brian
Cheung at Yahoo Finance.

44:49.646 --> 44:50.506 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

44:50.786 --> 44:51.666 align:middle
Hi, Chairman Powell.

44:51.666 --> 44:52.846 align:middle
Brian Cheung, Yahoo Finance.

44:52.926 --> 44:55.116 align:middle
I'm just wondering if you could
provide a little bit more color

44:55.116 --> 44:56.286 align:middle
in terms of how you're thinking

44:56.286 --> 44:58.376 align:middle
about mortgage-backed
securities purchases

44:58.376 --> 44:59.646 align:middle
as you inch towards taper.

44:59.766 --> 45:02.116 align:middle
Within the context of home
prices continuing to rise,

45:02.116 --> 45:04.936 align:middle
we've heard a lot of people talk
about the idea that maybe they'd

45:04.936 --> 45:07.706 align:middle
like to cool off on,
specifically, the MBS purchases.

45:07.706 --> 45:10.096 align:middle
Is that more because
of the optics,

45:10.096 --> 45:12.256 align:middle
or is it because there's
an observed relationship

45:12.256 --> 45:15.936 align:middle
from the Committee's view
between MBS purchases under QE

45:15.936 --> 45:17.076 align:middle
and the hot housing market?

45:17.076 --> 45:18.636 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

45:18.636 --> 45:22.916 align:middle
So a number of participants
raised that-the questions

45:22.916 --> 45:26.256 align:middle
around MBS and tapering
at, at today's meeting,

45:26.256 --> 45:28.536 align:middle
as a matter of fact,
and yesterday's meeting.

45:28.636 --> 45:32.156 align:middle
And I'll just say that,
generally speaking,

45:32.646 --> 45:36.896 align:middle
I don't think and-I don't think
that-I think that Treasury

45:36.896 --> 45:39.816 align:middle
and MBS purchases affect
financial conditions

45:39.816 --> 45:41.006 align:middle
in very similar ways.

45:41.386 --> 45:45.886 align:middle
There may be modest differences
in terms of, of contribution

45:45.886 --> 45:48.266 align:middle
to housing prices, but it's,
it's not something that's big.

45:48.266 --> 45:52.146 align:middle
It's not-it's-so where I
think we are is, there,

45:52.146 --> 45:55.426 align:middle
there really is little
support for the idea

45:55.426 --> 45:58.966 align:middle
of tapering MBS earlier
than Treasuries.

45:59.696 --> 46:02.156 align:middle
I think we will taper
them at the same time.

46:02.156 --> 46:05.516 align:middle
It seems likely, based
on where people are now.

46:06.316 --> 46:08.616 align:middle
The idea of reducing
MBS purchases

46:08.616 --> 46:10.136 align:middle
at a somewhat faster pace

46:10.196 --> 46:12.596 align:middle
than Treasuries does
have some attraction

46:12.596 --> 46:15.026 align:middle
for some people-others,
not so much.

46:15.416 --> 46:17.976 align:middle
And I think it's something that
we'll be continuing to discuss.

46:17.976 --> 46:18.586 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:20.986 --> 46:21.476 align:middle
Thank you.

46:21.476 --> 46:22.896 align:middle
Let's go to Michael Derby.

46:26.206 --> 46:26.826 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

46:26.826 --> 46:27.576 align:middle
Thank you very much.

46:27.576 --> 46:30.426 align:middle
So I wanted to ask you about
the standing repo facility

46:30.426 --> 46:34.436 align:middle
and get your sense of
what you think it will do

46:34.436 --> 46:36.906 align:middle
for market trading conditions.

46:36.906 --> 46:40.636 align:middle
And also, kind of in a
similarly related point,

46:40.636 --> 46:43.166 align:middle
the reverse repo numbers
have just gotten even bigger

46:43.166 --> 46:45.516 align:middle
since you raised the
reverse repo rate

46:45.516 --> 46:46.646 align:middle
at the last Fed meeting.

46:46.646 --> 46:48.876 align:middle
And, you know, are you
concerned to see, you know,

46:48.876 --> 46:50.906 align:middle
nearly $1 trillion
a day, you know,

46:51.056 --> 46:52.516 align:middle
pouring in through
that facility?

46:52.926 --> 46:54.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:54.496 --> 46:57.376 align:middle
So on the standing
repo facility,

46:57.476 --> 46:59.476 align:middle
what is it going to do?

46:59.476 --> 47:01.976 align:middle
So it, it really is a backstop.

47:02.136 --> 47:06.006 align:middle
So it's, it's set at 25 basis
points, so out of the money,

47:06.566 --> 47:09.316 align:middle
and it's, it's there to
help address pressures

47:09.316 --> 47:11.236 align:middle
in money market-money markets

47:11.296 --> 47:13.666 align:middle
that could impede the
effective implementation

47:13.666 --> 47:14.646 align:middle
of monetary policy.

47:14.926 --> 47:15.946 align:middle
So, really, it's, it's

47:15.946 --> 47:18.186 align:middle
to support the function
of-functioning

47:18.186 --> 47:21.876 align:middle
of monetary policy and
its, its effectiveness.

47:21.876 --> 47:23.026 align:middle
That's the purpose of it.

47:23.616 --> 47:27.806 align:middle
And it's set up with
that purpose in mind.

47:27.806 --> 47:31.046 align:middle
Your question on,
on the, the [ON]RRP,

47:31.766 --> 47:36.386 align:middle
so we think it's doing what it's
supposed to do, what we expect

47:36.386 --> 47:39.346 align:middle
of it to do, which is
to help provide a floor

47:39.346 --> 47:42.016 align:middle
for money market rates and help
ensure that the federal funds,

47:42.016 --> 47:44.866 align:middle
funds rate stays
within the target range.

47:44.866 --> 47:50.236 align:middle
You know, it's essentially,
essentially, what's happening is

47:50.386 --> 47:54.626 align:middle
that it just results in
a lower aggregate amount

47:54.626 --> 47:57.776 align:middle
of Federal Reserve liabilities
that are in banks in the form

47:57.776 --> 48:01.196 align:middle
of reserves and a higher amount
of Federal Reserve liabilities

48:01.656 --> 48:05.456 align:middle
in money market funds in the
form of overnight RRP balances.

48:05.546 --> 48:07.326 align:middle
So that, that's all that's
really happening there.

48:07.936 --> 48:10.956 align:middle
And we expect it to
be high for some time.

48:10.956 --> 48:13.376 align:middle
It's being driven,
of course, by,

48:13.746 --> 48:19.556 align:middle
by the relatively lower quantity
of Treasury bills and, and,

48:19.556 --> 48:22.106 align:middle
and also the onset of the
debt ceiling and the decline

48:22.296 --> 48:23.796 align:middle
in the TGA, things like that.

48:24.466 --> 48:27.156 align:middle
So we don't-we don't
have a problem with,

48:27.216 --> 48:27.996 align:middle
with what it's doing.

48:27.996 --> 48:29.576 align:middle
It's kind of doing
the job we expected.

48:29.576 --> 48:30.636 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

48:30.926 --> 48:33.606 align:middle
And you don't see any issues
with, like, disaggregating,

48:33.606 --> 48:34.796 align:middle
you know, markets
from, you know,

48:34.796 --> 48:36.936 align:middle
investing in private
money market securities?

48:36.936 --> 48:38.926 align:middle
You know, like, the idea
that the Fed's footprint

48:38.926 --> 48:40.506 align:middle
in money markets
is getting too big,

48:40.506 --> 48:41.716 align:middle
you don't see any issues there?

48:42.576 --> 48:42.826 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:42.826 --> 48:43.656 align:middle
Not, not really.

48:43.656 --> 48:44.496 align:middle
Not at this point.

48:44.996 --> 48:47.426 align:middle
I mean, money markets,
private money market funds are,

48:47.466 --> 48:49.266 align:middle
are choosing to, to invest

48:49.316 --> 48:50.736 align:middle
because the rates
are attractive.

48:50.856 --> 48:55.506 align:middle
At some point, the rates
will not be so attractive as,

48:55.606 --> 48:58.416 align:middle
as the whole rate
cluster normalizes, and,

48:58.416 --> 48:59.806 align:middle
and you'll see it
shrink back down.

49:00.246 --> 49:01.516 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

49:01.516 --> 49:01.946 align:middle
Okay. Thank you.

49:01.946 --> 49:02.896 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

49:02.896 --> 49:05.546 align:middle
Thanks. Let's go to Greg
Robb at MarketWatch.

49:09.656 --> 49:10.226 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

49:10.226 --> 49:11.766 align:middle
Hi. Thanks for taking
my question.

49:12.396 --> 49:15.456 align:middle
Chair Powell, I was going to go
back to inflation a little bit.

49:15.456 --> 49:17.086 align:middle
I'm just kind of
surprised by your tone.

49:17.086 --> 49:20.016 align:middle
It, it seems like you're
just sort of warning that,

49:20.016 --> 49:23.006 align:middle
if inflation gets too high,
you know, the Fed will act.

49:23.036 --> 49:27.076 align:middle
Isn't it true that, I mean,
a little inflation is good

49:27.076 --> 49:30.916 align:middle
for the economy and, and that
we-if somehow maybe we can get

49:30.916 --> 49:34.146 align:middle
the economy out of this sort of
place where we're always going

49:34.146 --> 49:37.716 align:middle
to be close to the zero lower
bound, isn't there a good story

49:37.716 --> 49:40.136 align:middle
to tell, and you're sort
of panicking people?

49:40.136 --> 49:40.366 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

49:40.366 --> 49:43.136 align:middle
I certainly don't,
don't have that in mind.

49:43.786 --> 49:49.716 align:middle
No. Look, we-as you know, we're
targeting a moderate overshoot

49:49.936 --> 49:52.046 align:middle
of 2 percent inflation
for some time.

49:52.666 --> 49:54.556 align:middle
We, we want inflation
expectations

49:54.556 --> 49:56.306 align:middle
to be centered on, on 2 percent.

49:56.306 --> 49:58.166 align:middle
We feel like they may be
a little bit below that.

49:58.476 --> 50:02.056 align:middle
So the bigger picture is
that, you know, that, that,

50:02.056 --> 50:03.056 align:middle
that would be a healthy thing.

50:03.316 --> 50:04.276 align:middle
This is a different thing.

50:04.276 --> 50:05.376 align:middle
This is-this is not that.

50:05.376 --> 50:07.376 align:middle
That was the kind of
inflation we were thinking

50:07.376 --> 50:10.196 align:middle
about that comes from,
you know, from a very,

50:10.196 --> 50:14.656 align:middle
very strong labor market and
a-and a booming economy maybe.

50:14.656 --> 50:16.186 align:middle
That, that was the
kind of inflation-this,

50:16.186 --> 50:17.976 align:middle
this is something different.

50:17.976 --> 50:21.426 align:middle
This is really driven by the
supply side, which is not able

50:21.426 --> 50:27.106 align:middle
to hand-to handle this big spike
in demand that we're seeing.

50:27.106 --> 50:31.496 align:middle
As the economy reopens with
vaccination and fiscal support

50:31.496 --> 50:34.846 align:middle
and monetary policy support,
the supply side-just all

50:34.846 --> 50:36.736 align:middle
over the world, you're seeing
the same thing, which is,

50:36.736 --> 50:38.126 align:middle
it just can't keep up.

50:38.646 --> 50:41.416 align:middle
And there are labor shortages
in, in a lot of places,

50:41.416 --> 50:42.486 align:middle
the same sort of thing.

50:42.566 --> 50:46.606 align:middle
So, you know, there's, there's
absolutely no sense of panic.

50:46.606 --> 50:49.246 align:middle
I just-I've explained, I
think, several times here today

50:49.246 --> 50:52.786 align:middle
that the best-my,
my best estimate is

50:52.786 --> 50:54.936 align:middle
that this is something
that will pass.

50:55.196 --> 50:58.286 align:middle
It's really a shock to the
economy that will pass through.

50:59.106 --> 51:01.066 align:middle
And, you know, if
you-if you look

51:01.066 --> 51:03.206 align:middle
at where forecasters
are-people who actually write

51:03.206 --> 51:05.046 align:middle
down a forecast for
a living-very,

51:05.046 --> 51:07.776 align:middle
very strongly they
see it that way.

51:08.386 --> 51:12.286 align:middle
Now, we-but, you know, are-we're
actually responsible for this,

51:12.286 --> 51:15.066 align:middle
though, so we have to
take seriously the risk,

51:15.536 --> 51:16.556 align:middle
the risk case, which is

51:16.556 --> 51:18.226 align:middle
that inflation will
be more persistent,

51:18.226 --> 51:20.926 align:middle
that it might actually move
inflation expectations up,

51:21.626 --> 51:24.196 align:middle
and that we might-that
the kind of things

51:24.196 --> 51:25.686 align:middle
that might require a response.

51:25.756 --> 51:27.976 align:middle
We, again, we don't
see that now.

51:27.976 --> 51:30.676 align:middle
But we, we have to be
on the alert for it.

51:30.676 --> 51:32.786 align:middle
And people have to
understand and believe

51:32.786 --> 51:35.296 align:middle
that we will react
if, if we need to.

51:35.776 --> 51:36.416 align:middle
And we will.

51:36.986 --> 51:40.116 align:middle
But, again, it's
not-it's not my base case.

51:40.116 --> 51:43.346 align:middle
My base case is that-is,
as I've said repeatedly,

51:43.346 --> 51:45.286 align:middle
is that inflation
will move back down.

51:46.016 --> 51:49.496 align:middle
And no, we're not-we, we have
not at all changed our view,

51:49.496 --> 51:53.116 align:middle
and I haven't changed my
view that inflation running

51:53.116 --> 51:54.756 align:middle
above 2 percent, moderately

51:54.756 --> 51:57.236 align:middle
above 2 percent, is
a desirable thing.

51:57.326 --> 51:59.156 align:middle
This is not moderately
above 2 percent.

51:59.156 --> 52:00.616 align:middle
This is well above 2 percent.

52:00.616 --> 52:03.136 align:middle
But it's also not the kind of
inflation we were looking for.

52:03.136 --> 52:05.716 align:middle
This is really driven
by a supply-side shock.

52:05.716 --> 52:06.316 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

52:07.786 --> 52:07.976 align:middle
Thank you.

52:07.976 --> 52:09.026 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

52:10.936 --> 52:11.326 align:middle
Thank you.

52:11.326 --> 52:13.736 align:middle
We'll go to Don Lee at
the Los Angeles Times

52:13.736 --> 52:15.046 align:middle
for the last question.

52:15.416 --> 52:16.766 align:middle
DON LEE. Hi, Chair Powell.

52:16.766 --> 52:20.136 align:middle
I wonder if you could
talk about wages.

52:20.376 --> 52:24.136 align:middle
Many workers seem to have
gotten some good wage gains in,

52:24.136 --> 52:26.386 align:middle
in recent months.

52:26.386 --> 52:29.066 align:middle
What can workers generally
expect going forward?

52:29.066 --> 52:31.866 align:middle
And it doesn't sound
like you're concerned

52:31.866 --> 52:36.216 align:middle
about rising wages feeding
into broader inflation.

52:36.216 --> 52:36.756 align:middle
Is that right?

52:37.816 --> 52:38.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

52:38.156 --> 52:39.856 align:middle
So wages have moved up.

52:40.836 --> 52:44.516 align:middle
A lot of that is
driven by new hires.

52:44.556 --> 52:48.556 align:middle
And it's-a lot of it is driven
at relatively low-paid jobs

52:48.596 --> 52:52.326 align:middle
in the service industries
as people come back.

52:52.326 --> 52:54.816 align:middle
So that's not troubling.

52:55.566 --> 52:59.416 align:middle
And we, we don't
see-there, there is a form

52:59.416 --> 53:02.616 align:middle
of wage inflation that can
lead to price inflation,

53:03.166 --> 53:04.656 align:middle
and we're not seeing
that right now.

53:04.656 --> 53:08.296 align:middle
And that really is if what we
call "unit labor costs" move

53:08.296 --> 53:14.346 align:middle
up and, and, and, which
really puts-move up in a way

53:14.346 --> 53:16.896 align:middle
that is-that is hard
for companies to manage

53:16.896 --> 53:18.416 align:middle
and puts them in a
situation where they have

53:18.416 --> 53:21.916 align:middle
to accept substantially lower
margins or raise prices.

53:22.566 --> 53:26.236 align:middle
Now, when it happens gradually,
when that-if you-we've seen

53:26.346 --> 53:31.266 align:middle
in a long expansion, sometimes
unit labor costs do move up and,

53:31.476 --> 53:33.976 align:middle
and put some pressure
on margins.

53:34.216 --> 53:37.286 align:middle
In a long expansion, that's been
happening late in the expansion.

53:37.286 --> 53:38.346 align:middle
That's not a problem, either.

53:38.716 --> 53:40.376 align:middle
The problem is if
it happens in a way

53:40.376 --> 53:44.806 align:middle
that pushes firms broadly
into raising prices.

53:44.806 --> 53:46.566 align:middle
It was called the
"wage-price spiral."

53:46.616 --> 53:47.826 align:middle
We don't see that now.

53:48.106 --> 53:49.986 align:middle
This is something
that was a feature

53:50.536 --> 53:55.206 align:middle
of the high inflationary
era of the Great Inflation,

53:55.206 --> 53:56.476 align:middle
but it's not a feature now.

53:56.476 --> 53:57.766 align:middle
And we don't see that now.

53:57.876 --> 53:59.216 align:middle
Of course, we'll be watching it.

53:59.696 --> 54:02.566 align:middle
And this is one of the reasons
why we're watching so carefully

54:03.216 --> 54:08.746 align:middle
to see whether labor
force-whether people do come

54:08.746 --> 54:10.126 align:middle
back and accept jobs.

54:10.126 --> 54:12.136 align:middle
Given the very large
number of job openings

54:12.616 --> 54:14.556 align:middle
and the very large number
of unemployed people,

54:14.556 --> 54:18.446 align:middle
we'd like to see, you know,
those-some matching going

54:18.446 --> 54:19.976 align:middle
on there so people
get back to work.

54:19.976 --> 54:24.076 align:middle
We think labor supply would
be-would be a healthy thing.

54:24.276 --> 54:26.616 align:middle
You know, but wages moving
up across the spectrum,

54:27.076 --> 54:28.206 align:middle
consistent with inflation

54:28.206 --> 54:31.106 align:middle
and productivity, is
a-is a good thing.

54:33.476 --> 54:34.096 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

54:34.826 --> 54:35.656 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

54:35.656 --> 54:36.226 align:middle
And thank you all.

